    "homepage_text": "Dr. Ming Sun's webpage\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nDr. Ming Sun's webpage\nHome\nRecent News:\nWe have two new XMM proposals approved in AO-25 in Dec. 2025 (\none in B: Sun; another in C: Tiwari\n)\nCheck the\nnew paper\non the XRISM stacking of ten galaxy clusters (what kept me busy for the last two months), also\nreported by the university on Dec. 16\nWe were granted XRISM time of\n200 ks in priority A on A1689 in AO-2\nCheck our\nnew paper\non the discovery of a long Halpha tail in the Virgo cluster and my simple model for the cloud deceleration\nThe\n1st XRISM Centaurus paper\nhas been\npublished\nby Nature (authors including Dr. Sun and Kokoro)!\nThe 1st XRISM A2029 paper\nhas been accepted by ApJL (authors including Dr. Sun and Prathamesh), more exciting results to come!\nDr. Sun has one ESO/MUSE proposal approved in P115 (B priority) as the science PI.\nDr. Sun has\ntwo\nnew\nXMM\nproposals approved in AO-2\n4\nin Dec. 202\n4\nas the PI,\n151 ks on three fields (C priority)\nOur recent paper on the Z8338 cluster is featured on the Chandra image release, link\n1\nand\n2\n.\nDr. Sun is\nhiring one new postdoc\n.\nContact me if interested.\nWe have one FAST proposal approved in priority A in the 2024 call (PI: Sun).\nDr. Sun's\n2023 NSF/AAG proposal\nwas approved, with a matching grant for the Czech collaborator in Czech!\nWe have one XRISM proposal in priority C approved in 2024 (PI: Sun).\nWe have one ALMA proposal approved in priority C in cycle 11 (7.2 hrs 12m + 20.5 hrs ACA, PI: Tamhane).\nDr. Sun has one HST GO proposal (6 orbits) approved in cycle 32 as the PI.\nDr. Sun has one ESO/MUSE proposal approved in P114 (B priority) as the science PI.\nOur new paper on X-ray cool core remnants\nhas been published.\nDr. Sun was awarded Research Excellence Award of 2024 in College of Science at the UAH\nDr. Sun's HST data\nwere\nfeatured in the ESA/Hubble Picture\nof the Week series\nsix\ntimes in 2024,\nOct. 21\n,\nJuly 29\n,\nApr. 29\n,\nMar 11\n,\nMar 4\nand\nFeb 19\n.\nDr. Sun has\none\nnew\nXMM\nproposal approved in AO-2\n3\nin\nDec.\n202\n3 as the PI,\n51 ks in B and 52 ks in C\nOur new paper on spectacular detached tails\nhas been published.\nWe welcome Dr. Juhi Tiwari and Dr. Prathamesh Tamhane to join Dr. Sun's group as new postdocs!\nWe have one\nMeerKAT\nproposal approved in the\n2023 open time call\n(37.5 hours on the Coma cluster, PI: Romana Grossova)\nWe have\none\nChandra\nproposal (A3017 for 168 ks, PI: Sun)\napproved in cycle 2\n5\nin 202\n3.\nOur new paper on the HST data of ESO 137-001\nhas been published.\nOur new paper with the full MUSE mosaic of ESO 137-001\nhas been published.\nDr. Sun's HST data featured in the\nESA/Hubble Picture of the Week series\n(the original selection came from the work by my previous RCEU student Andrew Guilory).\nDr. Sun was awarded 5 hours with DECam/Blanco from\nNOIRLab\nto have a deep Halpha survey of the Norma cluster.\nDr. Sun will give an invited talk in the \"\nThe X-ray Universe 2023\n\" conference in June 2023 at Athens, Greece.\nDr. Sun attended \"The 5th XRISM Science Meeting & XRISM core-to-core Multiwavelength Workshop 2022\" in Dec. 2022 in Tsukuba, Japan.\nDr. Sun is\nhiring two new postdocs\n(can work on\nXRISM\n,\nChandra\n,\nXMM\n,\nALMA\n,\nMeerKAT\n,\nHST\n,\nMUSE\n,\nSOFIA\nand all the exciting multi-wavelength data)\n! Contact me if interested.\nDr. Sun has three new\nXMM\nproposals approved in AO-22 in Nov. 2022 as the PI:\none priority B + two in priority C\n(259 ksec in total)\nDr. Sun was selected as one of the\nXRISM\nguest scientists\nto join the NGC 4636 team!\nWe were granted 15 hours of the\nMeerKAT\ntime (PI: Sun) on\nits 2022 open time call\n.\nDr. Sun has been promoted to Full Professor since August 2022.\nWe have two\nChandra\nproposals (PI: Sun) approved in cycle 24 in 2022 (one joint with\nHST\nand another joint with\nHST\n+\nVLA\n).\nWe have one\nHST\narchival proposal to study M87 (PI: Sun) approved in cycle 30 in 2022.\nCheck our\nnew review paper on ram pressure stripping\n.\nThe image from Our Nature Astronomy paper was chosen as cover image (and the hero image) of Nature Astronomy Feb. issue\n.\nA\npaper\non the spectacular central galaxy of A407 published on MNRAS.\nMolecular gas detected\nin the A1367 orphan cloud!\nOur new paper on\nthe correlation between hot and warm gas in stripped tails (\nSun et al. 2021\n) is published on\nNature Astronomy.\nA paper led by my graduate student Sunil Laudari is now published on\nMNRAS (\nLaudari et al. 2022\n)\n.\nDr. Sun is a co-I of a recently approved\nXMM\nlarge proposal on galaxy groups (PI: Dominique Eckert).\nOur group has four new\nXMM\nproposals approved in AO-21 in Dec. 2021 (\none in priority B + three in priority C\n).\nOur review paper on Ram Pressure Stripping (to be published on\nThe Astronomy and Astrophysics Review)\nis now posted on\narXiv (\nBoselli, Fossati & Sun 2022\n)\nOur new paper on the long X-ray tail + extension associated with the Blue Infall Group (BIG) in A1367 is published on MNRAS (\nGe, Sun et al. 2021\n).\nOur ALMA large proposal in cycle 8 (\nALMA JELLY\n) is approved (PI: Jachym; co-PI: Sun) with 61 hours of 12m time + 314 hours of 7m time!\nFour new\nChandra\nproposals\napproved in cycle 23 in July 2021!\nOur new paper on an X-ray/Halpha isolated cloud in A1367 published on MN\nRAS (\nGe, Luo, Sun et al. 2021)\n---\nsee the\nESA image release\n&\nUAH release\n, followed by many websites, including\nSky & Telescope\n.\nDr. Sun's\nSOFIA\nproposal\nin cycle 9 was approved (data all taken by Jan. 2022).\nOur group has\nfour\nnew\nXMM\nproposals approved in AO-2\n0\nin Dec. 202\n0\n(\nall\nin priori\nty C\n).\nOur new paper on the intriguing merging cluster Abell 2256 (shocks, cold fronts, radio halo and relic), with the deep\nChandra\nand\nXMM\ndata,  is now published on MNRAS (\nGe, Liu, Sun et al. 2020\n).\nOur new paper on the radio continuum and HI tails behind RPS galaxies in the Coma cluster (also with the deepest 1.4 GHz image on Coma) is published on MNRAS (\nChen, Sun et al. 2020\n).\nOur new paper on AGN feedback in one FR-II galaxy\nis published on\nMNRAS\n.\nThree proposals approved in\nXMM\nAO-\n19 (one B\n&\ntwo C)\nOur\nALMA\nresults on ESO 137-001 featured on ESO PoW\nOne proposal approved in\nChandra\ncycle 20 (PI: Sun)\nOur 3C88 paper was featured in the CfA weekly science highlight\nESO 137-001 was featured for NASA's James Webb Space Telescope\nD100 Hubble press release (\nHubble\nproject PI: Sun)\nDr. Sun\n's\nChandra\nC\nC\nT proposal approved\nand\nthis 1st observation\nhas been finished as the 1st cluster CAT target.\nDr. Sun\njoined the\nUS\nXMM\nuser group\n.\nDr. Sun\nwill be a tenured, associate professor from Aug., 2018\n.\nDr. Sun\nappears on\nScience chat for the STROBE-X mission\nTim Edge is selected for the 2018 CoS Undergraduate Department Achievement Award.\nDr. Sun\nis selected as the recipient of the UAH 2018 College of Science Outstanding Faculty Member Award.\nTwo proposals approved in\nXMM\ncycle 17, 94 ks A time, 73 ks B time and 38 ks C time (PI: Sun)\nTwo\nChandra\nproposals approved from my group (I: archive, PI: Morandi; II: GO, 130 ks, PI: Ge)\nOne\nHST\nproposal approved for five orbits (PI: Sun)\nDr. Sun was awarded one NSF/AAG grant on AGN feedback and galaxy evolution.\nDr. Sun received an extension on the NASA Alabama EPSCoR RID grant in 2017\nDr. Sun is one of the organizers of this new\nEWASS 2017 Symposium \"Ram pressure stripping and galaxy evolution\"\nin Prague at the end of June of 2017.\nTwo proposals approved in\nXMM\ncycle 16\n!\nA665 image now featured in the\n2016 Chandra Archive Collection\n(\nSarthak's paper\n)\nDr. Sun joined the X-ray Surveyor Science and Technology Definition Team:\nEvolution of Structure and AGN populations\n&\nPhysics of Plasmas\nDr. Sun will give the first talk (an invited review talk on local galaxy groups) in\nthis conference in Paris\nDr. Sun will give\nan invited colloquium at the U of Minnesota\nSample of Chandra ClustErs with Robust Shocks (SoCCERS)\nlaunched (update soon) !\nFour approved ALMA proposals in cy4 (one A + two B + one C, co-I: Sun)\nThree approved Chandra proposals in cycle 18\n: one GO (80 ks) + one archive (PI: Sun) + one archive (PI: Morandi)\nOur\nCFHT large project for a Halpha survey of the Virgo cluster\napproved for 50 nights ! (PI: Alessandro Boselli; Dr. Sun is one of the 43 co-Is and one of the two US co-Is)\nOne priority A PdBI proposal (lead co-I: Sun)\nAndrea's paper to constrain dark energy featured for a\nNASA press release\n, also covered\nlocally\nSarthak awarded an Alabama Commission on Higher Education Graduate Research Fellowship\nSarthak's paper on a strong Mpc shock in A665\naccepted by ApJL in 10 days!  The discovery will be followed by NuStar for 200 ks.\nOur\nATCA\nproposal to study AGN feedback on a southern cluster has been approved (PI: Sun) for\n22 hours\nSarthak's paper on 3C89\naccepted by MNRAS\nAndrea's paper to constrain dark energy\nwith cluster outskirts has been accepted by MNRAS\nDr. Sun was awarded a 2016 NASA Alabama EPSCoR RID seed grant\nSarthak won the 1st prize of the 8th\nWernher von Braun Memorial Symposium Student Poster Competition\n!\nSarthak's story\nfeatured on Gemini !\nNew results\non an ultra-massive black hole from IRAM-30m & PdBI\nTwo ALMA proposals approved in cycle 3 (one B + one C)\nBoth Andrea and Sarthak will talk in the IAU XXIX General Assembly in Hawaii on\nAug. 5, 2015\nTwo approved Chandra proposals in cycle 17 (PI: Sun, 245 ks Chandra + 3 orbits of HST + 23 hr VLA)\nDr. Sun was selected as a NASA endorsed member of the\nAthena\nStudy Science Team Working Groups (Athena is a next-generation X-ray telescope).\n10 hours with one of the largest radio telescope arrays in the world , GMRT, 28_021 (PI: Sun)\nAndrea's 1st paper on a large sample of\n320 clusters\nhas been accepted! More work to come!\nDr. Sun was invited for a review talk on galaxy groups in\nthis conference\n.\n2.8 hr priority A time with one of the biggest optical telescopes in the world !\nESO P95 time with\nVLT/MUSE\n(PI: Sun)\nTwo proposals approved in XMM cycle 14\n!\nproposal I: 36 ks A time + 92 ks B time + 23 ks C time (PI: Sun);\nproposal II: 69 ks B time (PI: Morandi)\nA successful LMT proposal on Perseus !\nTwo successful IRAM-...[truncated]"
  },
  "serper::Cheng Zhang": {
    "homepage": "https://czhang.org/",
    "emails": [
      "chengzhang@cornell.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Cheng Zhang (张铖)| SciFi Lab| Cornell\nCheng Zhang (张铖)| SciFi Lab| Cornell\nI am on sabbatical leave for the 2026 calendar year. If you are at Cornell and interested in research projects in my lab, please directly contact the\nPhD students\nin my lab.\nCheng Zhang is an Associate Professor (with Tenure) in Information Science and Computer Science (Field Member) at Cornell University, where he directs the\nSmart Computer Interfaces for Future Interaction (SciFi) Lab\n. He received his Ph.D. in Computer Science at Ubicomp Lab at the Georgia Institute of Technology, where he was advised by Dr. Gregory Abowd (CS) and Dr.Omer Inan(ECE).\nHis research focuses on integrating human-centered AI with advanced sensing technologies to empower everyday wearables in comprehending human behavior in real-world environments, with the goal of better supporting users. This work involves: (1) Creating low-power, minimally intrusive, and privacy-conscious everyday wearables that significantly lower the barrier to collecting high-quality human behavior data in natural settings; (2) Designing AI-powered sensing algorithms and systems that prioritize a human-centered understanding of behavior in everyday contexts; and (3) Advancing high-impact applications in areas like accessibility and health to better support users in their daily lives.\nHis research has already had\ntangible real-world impact\n. His early work on\nBeyondTouch\nand\nBackTap\nwas the first to demonstrate how tapping gestures on the phone can be recognized on off-the-shelf smartphones as input without additional hardware. Variants of this interaction are now available on millions of smartphones, including\nApple’s\nBack Tap feature\nintroduced in iOS 14 and later\n. In addition, another part of his prior research was licensed in 2016 to establish a Canadian startup ContextualHCI, based in Montreal, translating his academic innovations into practical technologies.\nHis work has been recognized with awards, including the\nNSF CAREER Award\n,\nUbicomp 10-year Impact Award\nas well as several Best Paper Awards and Honorable Mentions. His research is regularly featured by global media outlets including BBC, Forbes, Engadget, Fast Company, CNET, Gizmodo, New Scientist, the Verge, Digtal Trends, Popular Science, Designboom, and Mashable.\nRecent Professional Activities:\nSteering Committee (Co-)Chair\n:\nACM International Symposium on Wearable Computers (ISWC)\n(24-now)\nSteering Committee\n:\nACM international joint conference on Pervasive and Ubiquitous Computing(Ubicomp)\n(24-now)\nTechnical Program Committee (Co-)Chair\n:\nISWC’23, Ubicomp’24\nAssociate Chair/ Associate Editor\n:\nIMWUT(19-now), CHI(20,23,24,25)\nnews\nOct 13, 2025\nWill give a keynote talk on\nBeyondSound: The First Workshop on Intelligent Acoustic Sensing on Wearables\nat Ubicomp/ISWC’25, where I am also a co-organizer. Please consider attending this workshop with exciting programs and we can chat more!\nOct 09, 2025\nGoing to give a talk and particpiate a panel at\nDisability and Access in Tech and AI Summit\n. Happy to connect!\nSep 30, 2025\nHonored to received\nAnn S. Bowers’ 59 Research Excellence Awards\nfrom Bowers College of Computing and Information Science!\nSep 01, 2025\nExcited to give an an\ninvited talk at CS Seminar Series\nat CS department at University of Rochester on 9/15. Looking forward to visiting our neighbors in Rochester!\nJul 11, 2025\nEchoForce\nwas accepted to ISWC’25. The first system to recognize muscle activities by sensing sublte skin deformations with ultrasound with strong generalibility across remounting sessions.\nJul 11, 2025\nGrab-n-GO was accepted to IMWUT, which empowers always-availalbe interface on smartwatches by recognizing subtle finger gestures even if hands are occupied with different objects using AI-powered active acoustic sensing!\nJul 01, 2025\nOfficially promoted to Associate Professor with Tenure!\nJun 11, 2025\nOur paper\non exploring the impact of interpreting facial expressoins into ASL translation was accepted with minor revision to CSCW’25. First CSCW paper ever!\nMay 10, 2025\nOur workshop\nBeyondSound: The First Workshop on Intelligent Acoustic Sensing on Wearables\nhas been accepted to Ubicomp/ISWC’15. Please consider submitting your papers to this workshop or participating the workshop at Ubicomp’25.\nMay 05, 2025\nIEEE Spectrum wrote a\nstory\nabout SeamFit (IMWUT/Ubicomp’25).\nApr 14, 2025\nWill give an invited talk at\nUW ECE Research Colloquium Lecture Series on May 15\n, Hosted by\nDr.Yiyue Luo\n. First time visiting Seattle and University of Washington.\nApr 13, 2025\nWill Travel to Tokyo for CHI’25. Happy to chat if you are attending CHI.\nApr 10, 2025\nSeamPose (UIST’24)\nand\nSeamFit (IMWUT/Ubicomp’25)\nwas featured on\nCornell Chronicle\n,\nTechXplore\n,\nHackster\n,highlighting the future of AI-powered smart clothing that can sense human activity while remaining lightweight and comfortable.\nMar 20, 2025\nSpellRing (CHI’25)\nhas received global media coverage from leading tech and science outlets, such as\nCornell Chronicle\n,\nThe Verge\n,\nDesignboom\n,\nPopular Science\n,\nMental Floss\n,\nDigital Trends\n,\nHackster\n,\nTech Xplore\n,\nYanko Design\n,\nCommunications of the ACM\n,\nheise online\n,\nScience Daily\n,\nAssistive Technology Blog\n.\nJan 16, 2025\nSpellRing\nwas accepted with minor revision to CHI’25. A single ring that can recognize fingerspelling in ASL performed by native signers continously in real-time.\nJan 01, 2025\nSeamFit\nwas directly accepted with minor revision to IMWUT. Your smart shirt can now recognize your exercise.\nOct 10, 2024\nEchoGuide\nreceived ISWC’24 Best Paper Honorable Mention award.\nSep 26, 2024\nSonicID\nand\nActSonic\nare both accepted with minor revision to IMWUT. Smart glasses now can understand you activiites in the wild and know who you are with just 2 mic+speakers.\nSep 20, 2024\nRing-A-Pose\nis accepted with minor revision to IMWUT. The future of smart ring sensing can sense hand pose.\nSep 12, 2024\nTalked to\nWIRED\non the potential privacy challenge for the future of wearables.\nThe best way to reach out to me is through email."
  },
