  "serper::Zhongfei Zhang": {
    "homepage": "https://www.binghamton.edu/computer-science/people/profile.html?id=zzhang",
    "emails": [
      "zzhang@binghamton.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Zhongfei (Mark) Zhang - Our Faculty | School of Computing | Binghamton University\nSkip to main content\nBinghamton University: The State University of New York\nSchool of Computing\nsearch\nmenu\nSearch\nSchool of Computing\nOur Faculty\nZhongfei (Mark) Zhang\nProfessor\nSchool of Computing\nzzhang@binghamton.edu\n607-777-2935\nEB Q16\nBackground\nZhongfei (Mark) Zhang is a professor in the School of Computing at Binghamton University. He directs the Multimedia Research Laboratory at Binghamton.\nHe has a BS (cum laude) in electronics engineering, an MS in information science, both from Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, China, and a PhD in computer science from the University of Massachusetts at Amherst.\nWhile in the graduate school, he also worked as an Intern student at NEC Research Institute Inc. in Princeton, N.J., and as a technical consultant at Applied Artificial Intelligence Inc. (formerly Amerinex Artificial Intelligence Inc.) in Amherst, Mass.\nHe was a research scientist at the Center of Excellence for Document Analysis and Recognition and was on the faculty of the Department of Computer Science and Engineering, both at SUNY Buffalo, before he joined the faculty at Binghamton.\nHe also holds many visiting positions while he was on leave from Binghamton years ago, including a French government CNRS international chair professor at University of Lille 1 in France, a visiting research fellow at Microsoft Research, an NRC visiting fellow, a U.S. Air Force Research Laboratory visiting fellow, a Huang Kuancheng Foundation (in Hong Kong) visiting fellow at Chinese Academy of Sciences in China, a Japanese government JSPS fellow, a visiting faculty member at Carnegie Mellon University in the U.S. and Chuo University in Japan, a QiuShi chair professor at Zhejiang University in China, and an NSC visiting fellow at Academia Sinica in Taiwan.\nHe is the author of the first monograph on multimedia data mining and is an co-author of the first monograph on relational data clustering, both published by CRC Press. He has published over 200 peer-reviewed academic papers in premier international journals and conferences and several invited papers and book chapters in his areas, has edited or co-edited three books, is the inventor for more than 30 patents, has served as reviewers or program committee members or organization committee members for many international journals and conferences, and has served as grant review panelists for several governmental and private funding agencies, including the NSF and NASA.\nHis research is supported by federal government agencies, noticeably including NSF, AFOSR and AFRL as well as industrial research labs such as Microsoft Research and Kodak Research.\nHe is a fellow of IEEE, IAPR, AAIA, and National Academy of Inventors (NAI). He was invited to give keynotes or tutorials at premier international events in his areas. He is or was associate editor and guest editor for several international journals.\nWebsite\nEducation\nBS, MS, Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, China\nPhD, University of Massachusetts at Amherst\nResearch Interests\nMachine learning\nData mining and knowledge discovery\nMultimedia information indexing and retrieval\nComputer vision and image understanding\nPattern recognition\nAwards\nFellow of IEEE, IAPR, AAIA and NAI\nSUNY Chancellor's Award for Excellence in Scholarship and Creative Activities\nSUNY Chancellor's Award on Promising Inventor\nFrench Government CNRS Chair Professor\nJapanese Government JSPS Fellow\nU.S. National Academies/National Research Council Visiting Fellow\nU.S. Air Force Research Laboratory Visiting Fellow\nHuang Kuancheng Foundation (in Hong Kong) Visiting Fellow\nZhejiang University (in China) QiuShi Chair Professor\nNational Science Council (in Taiwan) Visiting Fellow\nMicrosoft Research Visiting Fellow\nSeveral Best Paper Awards and Service Awards from premier conferences\nReturn to Top\nLast Updated: 3/31/25"
  },
  "serper::Andrew M Dai": {
    "homepage": "https://marquisemergingleaders.com/andrew-m-dai-phd/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrewdai/",
    "homepage_text": "Andrew M. Dai, PhD - Emerging Leaders\nBack to all Leaders\nJuly 29, 2024\nTechnology\nAndrew M. Dai, PhD\nRELATED POSTS\nNathan C. Kaszei\nRead More »\nAndy J. Casas\nRead More »\nKevin Prior\nRead More »\nNAME: Andrew M. Dai, PhD\nTitle: Principal Research Scientist, Director\nCompany: Google\nEducation: PhD in Machine Learning, The University of Edinburgh (2012); BA in Computer Science, University of Cambridge (2006)\nCareer History: Principal Research Scientist, Director, Google (2012); Software Engineer Intern, Google (2008, 2009); Software Developer, Autonomy (2006-2007); ISAAS Intern, EY (2005)\nAndrew Dai, Principal Research Scientist and Director at Google, has been recognized as a Marquis Emerging Leader for their contributions and achievements in the field of technology.\nAttributing his success to blending his extensive research abilities with software engineering, Dr. Dai has excelled since 2012 in his role at Google, where he focuses on advancing technology and product development and wields particular expertise in large language models and machine learning. As a testament to his prominence there, he was recognized with a Tech Impact Award from the company in 2023. In the lead-up to this appointment, he gained experience as a software engineer intern at Google while earning a PhD in machine learning from the University of Edinburgh. He also previously worked as a software developer at Autonomy and an ISAAS intern at EY.\nGrowing up in China, Dr. Dai built on a fascination he had with the immersive world of video games to explore a passion for artificial intelligence in his high school years. This early intrigue with AI’s ability to simulate human-like behavior sparked a lasting interest that led him to pursue a career in this field. Upon graduating from high school, he obtained a bachelor’s degree in computer science at the University of Cambridge in England, where he authored a paper on the effectiveness of training and fine-tuning a language model to improve its ability to understand language, resulting in state-of-the-art performance. This paper was widely cited in subsequent GPT papers and published throughout Europe, having a far-reaching impact on the technology community.\nOutside of his primary responsibilities, Dr. Dai has served as an area chair and peer reviewer for NeurIPS, nCino, and ICOR conferences and regularly donates to various charities. He also holds a dedicated membership with the Association for Computing Machinery. Looking toward the future, Dr. Dai aspires to assume leadership roles in a company and establish a start-up. His plans include leveraging AI to make substantial contributions to global advancements.\nLinkedI\nn"
  },
