    "homepage_text": "Yangqiu Song\nI am now an associate professor at Department of CSE at HKUST and the associate director of HKUST-WeBank Joint Lab. I was an assistant professor at Lane Department of CSEE at WVU (2015-2016); a post-doc researcher at UIUC (2013-2015), a post-doc researcher at HKUST and visiting researcher at Huawei Noah's Ark Lab, Hong Kong (2012-2013); an associate researcher at Microsoft Research Asia (2010-2012); a staff researcher at IBM Research-China (2009-2010). I received my B.E. and PhD degree from Tsinghua University, China, in July 2003 and January 2009. I also worked as interns at Google in 2007-2008 and at IBM Research-China in 2006-2007.\nI am currently the PG coordinator of CSE and associate director of BSc in Data Science and Technology (DSCT)\nI am now also a visiting academic scholar at Amazon Rufus Team (Jan. 2022 - present).\nOffice: Room 3518\nHKUST, Clearwater Bay, Kowloon, Hong Kong\nEmail: yqsong # cse dot ust dot hk\nResearch Interests: Machine Learning, Data Mining, Natural Language Processing, Knowledge Graph and Information Networks.\nGoogle scholar\nDBLP\nLinkedIn\nRecent Research Topics (\nFull Publications (DBLP)\nand\nKnowComp Group Code\n):\nMy research goal is to use machines to understand human languages and eventually facilitate natural human language interaction and communication. Our current approach is to extract useful knowledge from existing resources to construct knowledge graphs and use knowledge graphs to do reasoning and inference for understanding. When meeting downstream applications, we also need to care about privacy, security, and fairness issues to build trustworthy real-world systems.\nKnowledge graph/base construction (\nNew Trends\n)\nProBase\n: A concept knowledge graph extracted from billions of Web pages (\nlatest version\n)\nASER\n: An eventuality knowledge graph of activities, states, events, and their relations\nTransOMCS\n: Population of a commonsense knowledge base in the format of OMCS transferred from linguistic patterns in ASER\nDISCOS\n: Population of ATOMIC if-then inferential commonsense knowledge base with the discourse relations in ASER (\nExtension: CSKB Population Benchmark\n)\nAbstractATOMIC\n: A conceptualization framework of commonsense knowledge aquisition and its resulting abstract knowledge in the form of ATOMIC\nAbsPyramid\n: An implementation driven by Marvin Minsky's K-Line theory; a unification of entity typing, event typing, conceptualization, and entailment graphs\nAutoSchemaKG\n: A framework to automatically extract entity, event, and perform conceptualization at Web scale, with three large examples: ATLAS-Wiki (from Wikipedia), ATLAS-Pes2o (from academic papers), and ATLAS-CC (from Common Crawl)\nMARS\n: A metaphysical reasoning knowledge base\nFolkScope\n: Eventuality-based intention knowledge graph for e-commerce commonsense understanding (later extented to\nAmazon COSMO\n)\nNatural language understanding and grounding to knowledge graphs\nConceptualization of the world knowledge\nEntity and event linking, semantic parsing, and discourse analysis\nMachine learning, inference, and reasoning with world knowledge\nWorld knowledge representation, learning, and inference\nLearning with global structural world knowledge\nNeuro-symbolic inference and reasoning\nNeural graph database/knowledgebase systems\nLeveraging (commonsense) knowledge and social norms to improve privacy, security, explainability, and fairness issues in machine learning models for NLP\nTalks:\n2025 Summer: Huawei.\nTowards Compliant LLM Agents: From Contextual Integrity (CI) to Model Contextual Integrity Protocol (MCIP)\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n]\n2024 December: WeBank.\nAI Privacy & Safety Compliance: From Checklist to Reasoning\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n]\n2024 July: Federated Learning Summer School, Qingdao.\nPrivacy-Preserved Neural Graph Databases\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n]\n2024 May: WWW 2024 Tutorial.\nNew Frontiers of Knowledge Graph Reasoning: Recent Advances and Future Trends\n. [\nLink\n]\n2024 April: HKUST.\nE-Commerce Commonsense Knowledge Graphs for Intention-based Recommendation\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n]\n2023 November: Amazon/NVIDIA/Huawei/WeBank.\nComplex Query Answering on Neural Knowledge Graphs with Rich Semantics\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n]\n2023 October: COMP5222 Lectures on\nComplex Query Answering on Neural Knowledge Graphs\n. [\nPart 1\n], [\nPart 2\n] (Most contents are developed by\nZihao Wang\n(\norginal slides\n).\n2023 Summer: IJCAI-FL.\nPrivacy Attacks on Large Language Models\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n]\n2023 Summer: KDD-China.\nActivity (or Process), State, and Event-based Knowledge Graphs\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n]\n2022 July: Amazon Search Science Team.\nAcquiring and Modeling Abstract Commonsense Knowledge via Conceptualization\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n]\n2021 Novermber: CCKS Tutorial.\nCommonsense Knowledge Acquisition and Reasoning\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n]\n2021 September: Huawei Workshop on Commonsense.\nCommonsense Knowledge Base Population\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n]\n2021 Summer: WeBank, BUPT, and 4Paradigm.\nDifferentially Private Federated Knowledge Graphs Embedding\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n]\n2021 Summer: Renmin University and THU.\nAn Overview of Commonsense Knowledge Graph Construction and Reasoning at HKUST\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n]\n2020 KDD Tutorial.\nRecent Advances on Graph Analytics and Its Applications in Healthcare\n. [\nTutorial Page\n] [\nPart 3 ppt\n]\n2020 July: Knowledge Works at Fudan and THU.\nASER: Building a Commonsense Knowledge Graph by Higher-order Selectional Preference\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n]\n2019 July: HIT Event Reasoning Workhop, BUPT, PKU, Beihang.\nASER: A Large scale Eventuality Knowledge Graph\n. [\npdf\n], [\nppt\n], [\nWWW20 Version\n]\n2018 November: ICDM Workshop on Large Scale Graph Representation Learning and Applications (GRLA).\nAutomatic Information Fusion with Heterogeneous Information Networks\n. [\nppt\n] [\npdf\n]\n2017 Summer: SCUT, PKU.\nRecent Development of Heterogeneous Information Networks: From Meta-paths to Meta-graphs\n. [\nppt\n] [\npdf\n]\n2016 IJCAI Workshop on Heterogeneous Information Network Analysis (HINA).\nIncorporating Structured World Knowledge into Unstructured Text via Heterogeneous Information Networks\n. [\nppt\n] [\npdf\n] (extended abstract [\npdf\n])\n2015 ICDM Workshop on Practical Transfer Learning.\nText Classification without Supervision: Incorporating World Knowledge and Domain Adaptation\n. [\nppt\n] [\npdf\n]\nGroup:\nPostdocs:\nHaoran Li\n(Since Spring 2025)\nJiaxin Bai\n(Since Spring 2025)\nXi Yang\n(Since Spring 2025)\nPhDs:\nHaoyu Huang\n(Since Fall 2025)\nYufei Li (Since Fall 2025)\nXiyu Ren (Since Fall 2025)\nYuxuan Liu (Since Fall 2025)\nAndy Yau Wai YIM (Since Fall 2025)\nTsz Ho CHAN (Part-time, Since Fall 2025)\nHuihao Jing\n(Since Spring 2025)\nWenbin Hu (Since Spring 2025)\nTianshi Zheng\n(Since Fall 2024)\nBaixuan Xu\n(Since Fall 2024)\nQing Zong\n(Since Fall 2024)\nHaochen Shi (Since Fall 2024)\nWei Fan\n(Since Fall 2023)\nZhaowei Wang\n(Since Fall 2023)\nJiayang Cheng\n(Since Fall 2023)\nJacky Chun Kit Chan\n(Since Fall 2023)\nWeiqi Wang\n(Since Fall 2022)\nZheye Deng\n(Since Fall 2022)\nZizheng Lin (Since Fall 2019)\nMPhils:\nChangxuan Fan\n(Since Fall 2025)\nJerry, Ki Sen HUNG(Since Fall 2025)\nAnson, Tsun On KWOK(Since Fall 2025)\nKa Wai CHUNG(Since Fall 2025)\nYisen Gao\n(Since Fall 2025)\nZhongwei Xie (Since Fall 2025)\nBowen Liu (Since Fall 2025, Co-supervised with Xiaomeng Li)\nShurui Zhang (Since Fall 2025, Co-supervised with Kani Chen)\nXingyan Chen (Since Fall 2024, Co-supervised with Kani Chen)\nChunyang Li\n(Since Fall 2024)\nLucas Wun Yu CHAN\n(Since Fall 2024)\nJason Tsz Ho LI (Since Fall 2024)\nDennis Hong Ting TSANG\n(Since Fall 2024)\nAsif KHAN\n(Since Fall 2024)\nPeng Xie (Since Fall 2024, Co-supervised with Kani Chen)\nHongyu Luo (Since Fall 2024)\nZirui Wang (Since Fall 2022, Co-supervised with Qian Xu)\nPast students, interns, visitors, collaborators with first employment:\nHKUST PhD:\nQi Hu (Since Fall 2020-Spring 2025, First Employment: Byte Dance in Shanghai). Thesis: Privacy-Preserved Learning on Graph-Structured Data\nJiaxin Bai\n(Fall 2020-Spring 2025, First Employment: Postdoc@HKUST). Thesis: Advancements in Knowledge Graph Reasoning: Innovative Approaches to Complex Query Answering and Logical Hypothesis Generation\nZihao Wang\n(Fall 2020-Spring 2025, First Employment: Financial Industry in Hong Kong). Thesis: Querying Neural Graph Databases\nYing Su\n(Fall 2020-Fall 2024, First Employment: Postdoc@HKU). Thesis: Commonsense Knowledge Representation, Reasoning and Application\nHaoran Li\n(Fall 2020-Fall 2024, First Employment: Postdoc@HKUST). Thesis: Enhancing Trustworthiness in Large Language Models: Perspectives on Privacy and Safety\nTianqing Fang\n(Fall 2019-Summer 2024, First Employment: Tencent AI Lab in Shenzhen). Thesis: Commonsense Knowledge Base Population and Reasoning for Inferential Knowledge\nXin Liu\n(Fall 2018-Summer 2023, First Employment: Scientist@Amazon A9 in Palo Alto). Thesis: Theory and Practice: Graph Representation Learning For Subgraph Isomorphisms and Substructure-Augmented Applications\nHuiru Xiao\n(Fall 2016-Summer 2022, First Employment: Lecturer@HKUST). Thesis: Learning with Hierarchical Data\nWenyi Xiao\n(Fall 2016-Fall 2021, First Employment: Scientist@Huawei in Hong Kong). Thesis: Path Learning in Complex Networks\nHongming Zhang\n(Fall 2018-Summer 2021, First Employment: Scientist@Tencent AI Lab in Seattle). Thesis: Towards Commonsense Reasoning with Higher-order Selectional Preference over Eventualities\nHongliang Dai\n(Fall 2017-Summer 2021, First Employment: Scientist@Tencent Jarvislab in Shenzhen). Thesis: Weak Supervision for Information Extraction\nZiqian Zeng\n(Fall 2016-Summer 2021, First Employment: Assistant Professor@SCUT in Guangzhou). Thesis: On the Use of Auxiliary Information for Label-less Text Mining Tasks\nYinghua Zhang (Fall 2015-Spring 2021, Co-supervised with Prof. Qiang Yang, First Employment: Research Engineer@Meituan in Shanghai). Thesis: Deep Transfer Learning: Generalization on Clean and Adversarial Data\nNedjma Ousidhoum\n(Fall 2014-Summer 2021, Co-supervised with Prof. DY Yeung, First Employment: Postdoc@Cambridge). Thesis: On the Importance of Normalization of the...[truncated]"
