  "serper::Anru R. Zhang": {
    "homepage": "https://anruzhang.github.io/",
    "emails": [
      "anru.zhang@duke.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Anru Zhang's Homepage\nAnru Zhang\nDepartment of Biostatistics & Bioinformatics and Departments of Computer Science, Duke University\nHome\nResearch\nPublications\nGroup Members\nTeaching\nSoftware\nBackground\nI am a tenured faculty member with joint primary appointments in the Department of\nBiostatistics & Bioinformatics\nand the Department of\nComputer Science\nat\nDuke University\n.\n  I was an assistant professor of statistics at UW-Madison before joining Duke.\n  I received my PhD from the University of Pennsylvania in 2015 (advised by T. Tony Cai) and my bachelor's degree in mathematics from Peking University in 2010.\nI serve as:\nAssociate Chair for Research in the Department of Biostatistics & Bioinformatics at Duke;\nAssociate Editor for\nThe Annals of Statistics\n;\nAssociate Editor for\nJournal of the American Statistical Association (Applications & Case Studies)\n;\nAssociate Editor for\nJournal of the American Statistical Association (Theory & Methods)\n;\nAssociate Editor for\nStatistica Sinica\n;\nAssociate Editor for\nStatistics and Its Interface\n;\nAssociate Editor for\nASA Discoveries\n;\nAssociate Editor for\nStatistical Learning and Data Science\n;\nI won the\nLeo Breiman Junior Award\n(2026),\nCOPSS Emerging Leader Award\n(2024),\nIMS Tweedie Award\n(2022), as featured here in\nIMS News\n,\n  the\nASA Gottfried E. Noether Junior Award\n(2021), as featured\nhere\n,\n  the\nNSF CAREER Award\n(2020),\n  and the AMIA Data Science Outstanding Paper Award (2023)\n  (\nNews\n).\n  At Duke, I have been recognized as the Eugene Anson Stead, Jr. M.D. Associate Professor and received a\nTeaching Excellence Recognition\n.\nSome keywords of my research: generative AI, tensor learning, electronic health records, healthcare, microbiome studies, high-dimensional statistics, ...\n  You can find a list of our group's publications from the tab above, on\narXiv\n,\n  or on\nGoogle Scholar\n.\nFor prospective PhD students:\nPlease apply to the PhD programs in\nBiostatistics & Bioinformatics\n,\nComputer Science\n,\nElectrical & Computer Engineering\n,\n  or\nCBB Program\n.\n  Prospective PhD students interested in working with me can mention this on their applications.\n  Applying to multiple programs at Duke increases your chances and is allowed.\nOur group is actively looking for motivated members to work on (1) methodology and theory in machine learning and statistics and/or (2) applications in AI Health.\nNews\n[4/2026] I will teach a short course on generative models at Duke Industry Statistics Symposium (DISS) on April 8, 2026.\n[3/2026] I am co-teaching a short course on \"Generative Models for Protein Structures and Biomedical Data\" with Alex Tong and Fred Peng at ENAR (Indianapolis, IN) on March 15, 2026. All are welcome! Course website\nhere\n.\n[2/2026] I was honored to receive the\n2026 Leo Breiman Junior Award\nfrom the ASA Section on Statistical Learning and Data Science (SLDS). Named in honor of\nDr. Leo Breiman\n, a pioneering figure in machine learning whose foundational contributions include CART, bagging, and random forests, this award recognizes outstanding theoretical or methodological contributions to machine learning and computational statistics with substantial impact on both the field and practical applications. I am especially inspired by Dr. Breiman's influential perspective on the\n\"two cultures\"\nof statistical modeling, which helped shape modern data science.\n[2/2026] I was honored to receive the\n2026 Leo Breiman Junior Award\nfrom the ASA Section on Statistical Learning and Data Science (SLDS). Named in honor of\nDr. Leo Breiman\n, a pioneering figure in machine learning whose foundational contributions include CART, bagging, and random forests, this award recognizes outstanding theoretical or methodological contributions to machine learning and computational statistics with substantial impact on both the field and practical applications. I am especially inspired by Dr. Breiman's influential perspective on the\n\"two cultures\"\nof statistical modeling, which helped shape modern data science.\n[2/2026]\nPlanner aware path learning in diffusion language models training\nwas accepted as an oral presentation at ICLR 2026!\n[1/2026] I was honored to receive a Teaching Excellence Recognition from the Duke University School of Medicine for BIOSTAT 915 (High-Dimensional Statistics), recognized as both a Top Instructor and a Top-Rated Course in Fall 2025.\nNews\n[10/2025] Our paper\nWhy Do Transformers Fail to Forecast Time Series In-Context?\nwas selected as an oral presentation (3 out of 68 submissions) for\nWhat Can('t) Transformers Do? @ NeurIPS 2025\n.\n[4/2025] My former PhD advisee, Yuetian Luo, has accepted an offer to become a\ntenure-track Assistant Professor in the Department of Statistics, Rutgers University\n. Congratulations!\n[2/2025] The former visiting student in our group, Jingyang Li, has accepted a position as an Assistant Professor in the\nDepartment of Statistics & Data Science at the School of Management, Fudan University\n, starting in January 2026. Congrats!\n[12/2024] PhD student Shiyi Jiang has successfully defended her PhD thesis. She will join the Department of Biomedical Informatics at Columbia University Medical Center as a postdoctoral research fellow after graduation. Congrats!\nJoin us to celebrate Ed Hammond! Ed Hammond 90th Celebratory Scientific Symposium \"From Foundations to Future of Informatics in Health.\" January 9–10, 2025, Washington Duke Inn, Durham, NC. For more information, visit the\nEd Hammond Symposium website\n.\n[03/2024] My former PhD advisee, Yuchen Zhou, has accepted an offer to become a\ntenure-track Assistant Professor in the Department of Statistics, University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign\n. Congratulations!\n[03/2024] I was extremely honored and humbled to receive the\nCOPSS Emerging Leader Award\n. This award recognizes early career statistical scientists who show evidence of and potential for leadership and who will help shape and strengthen the field.\n[01/2024]\nRunshi Tang\nhas received an honorable mention award in the 2024 Student Paper Competition of the ASA Statistical Learning and Data Science Section for his paper \"\nMode-wise Principal Subspace Pursuit and Matrix Spiked Covariance Model\n.\" His paper stood out among 79 submissions this year. Congrats!\n[03/2023] Our paper \"Timeline registration for electronic health records\" won the\nData Science Distinguished Paper Award\nfrom the 2023\nAMIA Informatics Summit\n(only one paper received this award). (\nNews\n)\n[10/19/2022] My recently graduated PhD student\nYuetian Luo\nwon the\n2023 IMS Lawrence D. Brown Award\n(this distinction is given to three PhD students in the IMS community)! Yuetian will deliver an award lecture at the Joint Statistical Meetings in Toronto, Canada in August 2023. (\nIMS News\n)\n[8/2022] I received the\nIMS Tweedie Award\n(2022) (\nIMS News\n) and presented at the IMS New Researcher Conference. This award is given by the Institute of Mathematical Statistics to a single young researcher each year (\nprevious awardees\n).\n[5/2022] Yuetian graduated and will join the\nUniversity of Chicago Data Science Institute\nas a postdoctoral fellow starting in 7/2022.\n[10/26/2021] My PhD student\nRungang\n, as one of three recipients in the IMS community, won the\n2022 IMS Lawrence D. Brown Award\n! Rungang will deliver an award lecture at the\n2022 IMS Annual Meeting in London\n.\nContact Information\nE-mail:\nanru.zhang@duke.edu\nHome\n|\nResearch\n|\nPublications by Topics\n|\nGroup Members\n|\nTeaching\n|\nSoftware\nCopyright © Anru Zhang| Designed by\nsimplestyle_3"
  },
