  "serper::Zhecan Wang": {
    "homepage": "https://www.zhecanwang.com/",
    "emails": [
      "olinzhecanwang@gmail.com"
    ],
    "github": "https://github.com/ZhecanJamesWang",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/jameszhecanwang/",
    "homepage_text": "Zhecan (James) Wang\nMenu\nZhecan (James) Wang\nStreet Address\nCity, State, Zip\nPhone Number\nInnovation and Passion Driven\nMultimodal Representation, Commonsense Reasoning\nZhecan (James) Wang\nContact Email: olinzhecanwang[at]gmail.com\nI am Zhecan (James) Wang, a\nPostdoctoral Research Fellow\nin the\nNatural Language Processing\nGroup of Computer Science at the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA), working under the mentorship of Prof. Kai-Wei Chang (Amazon Scholar and Sloan Fellow) and Prof. Nanyun Peng since September 2024 ( I expect to complete this 1-year contract by fall 2025). I completed my Ph.D. in Computer Science at Columbia University in August 2024, where I had the privilege of being primarily advised by Prof. Shih-Fu Chang (Morris A. and Alma Schapiro Professor, Dean of the Columbia Engineering, a Fellow of the National Academy of Engineering and ACM). Meanwhile, I was also fortunate to benefit from the co-advisement of Prof. Kai-Wei Chang at UCLA-NLP. During my Ph.D., I spent almost two years conducting collaborated research projects at\nGoogle Brain/DeepMind\n, working directly in the multimodal (Gemini) team led by Dr. Quoc V. Le. In addition, I have also been involved in a 1-year collaborative research project at\nMicrosoft Research\nas an intern/part-time researcher under the multimodal team led by Dr. Lu Yuan.\nResearch Interest:\n[In One Sentence] My past works have mainly focused on improving\nMultimodal Learning\nand resolving\nMultimodal Commonsense Reasoning\nproblems from the\nNatural Language Processing\nperspective, primarily focusing on\nExplainable, Reliable\n, and\nTrustworthy Reasoning\nand\nNeural-Symbolic learning\n.\n[In Multiple Sentences] My research during my Ph.D. program has been funded by the\nDARPA\nMachine Common Sense (MCS) Program. It has focused on artificial intelligence, particularly\nNatural Language Processing\n,\nMultimodal Learning\n(e.g., vision and language.),\nCommonsense Reasoning\n,\nNeural-Symbolic Learning\n, and\nHuman-in-the-Loop\n. Some of my recent works also emphasize\nTrustworthy\nand\nExplainable\nAI,\nHallucination Mitigation\n,\nRigorous Multimodal Mathematics Reasoning\n, Unified\nMultimodal\nAgents\n,\nSecurity in (Agent) Reasoning\n, and\nEmbodied\nLearning. I am expanding my current and future research direction to\nInteractive Learning\n(e.g., RL) with\nSparse Multimodal Human Feedback\n,\nHuman-AI Interaction/System\n,\nHuman-centered AI\n, and\nAI for Health/X\n.\nAcademic Excellence and Leadership:\nMy collaborations extend across academia and industry, working closely with 17 professors from 12 institutions across multiple DARPA projects. As a principal contributor to the\nDARPA MCS\n(Machine Commonsense) and\nECOLE\n(Environment-driven Conceptual Learning) programs, I presented directly to DARPA managers during annual evaluations. I've led significant collaborative projects with top universities, achieving\ntop performance\non the\nDARPA Visual Commonsense Reasoning leaderboard\nand\nSOTA\nperformance on VCR, VQA v2, SNLI-VE, A-OKVQA, and OKVQA. My leadership skills were further highlighted by guiding a 12-student team to\nfirst place\nin the ICCV Microsoft Global MS-Celeb-1M Challenge. Currently, I am also applying as a\nCo-PI\nfor\nindustry funds\nlike the\nGoogle Research Scholar\nProgram\nand AI for Math Fund from Renaissance Philanthropy\n.\nMoving forward, I aim to foster new research partnerships and pursue funding opportunities from agencies like DARPA, NSF, and NIH, as well as industry collaborations, to advance multimodal commonsense reasoning and related fields.\nSince beginning my AI research in 2014, I've published\n16 papers\nin top-tier conferences and\n7\nin top-tier workshops, secured\n8 AI-related patents\n, and amassed\n1000+ citations\n. At Columbia, I have contributed\n10\npapers to prestigious venues, serving as\nthe first or co-first author\n, and my work has been acknowledged by organizations such as\nPaperWeekly, AI2\n,\nDARPA,\nand so on.\nAwards and Recognition:\nMy academic journey has been marked by significant achievements, including receiving the ECCV-24 Doctoral Consortium, AAAI-22 / BMVC-17 Student Scholarship, and leading my team to victory in the ICCV MS-Celeb-1M challenge. These accolades, alongside my research consistently delivering\nstate-of-the-art\nresults over multiple Vision-Language Understanding benchmarks, underscore my capability for making groundbreaking contributions to the field.\nService to the Department and Community:\nAs Head Representative for the Computer Science Department at the Engineering Graduate Student Council, I've led housing transparency initiatives and organized professional events, underscoring my commitment to a vibrant academic community. Additionally, I serve as the reviewer and Program Committee Member for conferences like CVPR, AAAI, etc., and as a research committee member for Columbia's In Transcription Club.\nPrevious Professional and Internship Experience:\nBefore joining Columbia, I worked as a full-time AI researcher at XPeng Motors’ research center in Silicon Valley, where I was the only undergraduate full-time staff member accepted. During this time, I was directly mentored by Dr. Yan Dongguo (former Senior Researcher at Microsoft Research). Before that, I was guided at the LV lab at the National University of Singapore by Prof.Jiashi Feng and Prof. Ashraf Kassim. During this period, I participated in a collaborative project between Panasonic's AI Lab and NUS and won first place in all categories of Microsoft's 2017 One-Million Celebrity Human Face Competition.\nEarly Educational Background:\nI received my undergraduate degree in Computer Engineering from Franklin W. Olin College of Engineering in 2018 (a young engineering school spun out of MIT and ranked third in undergraduate engineering education by US News) as the fourth International Chinese student in the school history. During my undergraduate years, I received a four-year tuition scholarship and was the campus ambassador for collaboration among three colleges (Babson, Olin, and Wellesley Colleges). Thanks to the school's cooperative mechanism, I also had the freedom to explore cross-registration at MIT, Wellesley College, and Babson College.\nLeadership\nLead Department Representative at the Engineering Graduate Student Council (EGSC).\nCo-founder of HackIT, an online non-profit engineering education platform\nHonor\n1st place\nin all sub-categories of ICCV 2017 Microsoft Global Challenge of Recognizing One Million Celebrities in the Real World (MS-Celeb-1M) hosted by\nMicrosoft Research\n.\nOne of the world’s top competitions\nwith the largest data set for large-scale classification and low-shot problem\nReceiver of AAAI-2022 Student Scholarship Program\nReviewer\nof ICCV 2019, NeurIPS 2019, AAAI 2019, CVPR 2020, ECCV 2020, AAAI 2020, NeurIPS 2020, WACV 2020, ACCV 2020, AAAI 2021, CVPR 2021, ICCV 2021, ARR 2021, NeurIPS 2022, AAAI 2022, WACV 2022, EMNLP 2022, CVPR 2022, MM 2022, ARR 2022, ICLR 2023, AAAI 2023, MM 2023, NeurIPS 2023, AAAI 2023, WACV 2023, EMNLP 2023, ARR 2023, AAAI 2024,  WACV 2024 and ICLR 2024.\nInvited to meet the\nPrime Minister of Denmark\nand participate in the cultural exhibition.\n1st place\nat the 2nd Facial Landmark Localisation Competition - The Menpo BenchMark, hosted by iBUG (Intelligent Behaviour Understanding Group), Imperial College London\nTravel Grant\nat the British Machine Vision Conference (\nBMVC\n) 2017\nRecipient of 4-year Olin College Tuition Scholarship\nTwo Golden Key and two Silver Key Awards\n(\nState-level\n) for oil painting and carving\nOutstanding Intern Reporter Award\n, Strait Metropolis Daily (the biggest press  in southeastern China)\nReference\nGoogle Scholar\nLinkedin\nGithub\nPatent\nEducation\nColumbia University\nDoctoral program\n(current)\nMaster of Science\nOlin College of Engineering\nOriginated from MIT;\nRanked by MIT Study as the Global State of the Art in Engineering Education;\nRanked as the top 3 best undergraduate engineering school by U.S. News;\n4th Chinese International student in school’s history.\nBachelor of Science in Engineering, with a Concentration in Computing\nNational University of Singapore\n(exchange and visiting UROP research)\nMIT, Wellesley College\nand\nBabson College\n(cross-registration)\nAmerican Heritage School\n(Top 10 private high school and #1 for math in the U.S. )\nSlagelse Gynasium (\nbiggest high school in the mid Denmark area)\nPassionate about\nMachine Learning, Multi-Media and Robotics\nEntrepreneurship, Product/Market Fit\nBeing Creative and Making Impacts\nInteresting Facts of me\nWas visited privately by\nPrime Minister of Denmark\nWrote a book about my gap year of hitch-hacking and volunteering\nHad been one of the very few male students at Wellesley College\nZhecan (James) Wang\nPostdoc Research Fellow at UCLA-NLP\nCS Ph.D. at Columbia University\n​​Part-time ​Graduate Researcher at Google Deepmind (Brain)\nFormer Contract Researcher at Microsoft Research\nFormer Department Representative at EGSC"
  },
