  "serper::Marvin Zhang": {
    "homepage": "https://www.wrzlab.org/",
    "emails": [
      "zhan6033@purdue.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Home | W R Zhang Lab\ntop of page\nW-R Zhang Lab @ Purdue\nOur focus\nAt the W-R Zhang Lab @ Purdue University (Departments of Biological Sciences and Computer Science), we combine spatial multi-omics, imaging, and clinical data with machine learning and AI to study how tumors organize and evolve, bridging biology and computation where interdisciplinary research sparks breakthroughs.\nRecent research highlights\nComputational framework to link functional enrichment gradient with cellular composition\nin the tumor microenvironment, providing new\ninsights into tumor progression.\nMONTAGE\n: A Computation Framework to Identify Spatially Resolved Functional Enrichment Gradients in the Tissue Microenvironment via Spatial Communities.\nPreprint on bioRxiv. Zhang et al. (2025)\nhttps://doi.org/10.1101/2025.04.30.651486\nMachine learning algorithm to single-cell spatial omics,  breaking analytical barriers to uncover biomarkers for cancer lymph node metastasis.\nIdentification of cell types in multiplexed in situ images by combining protein expression and spatial information using\nCELESTA\n.\nNature methods.\nZhang et al.\n(2022)\nhttps://www.nature.com/articles/s41592-022-01498-z\nbottom of page"
  },
  "serper::Hongteng Xu": {
    "homepage": "http://hongtengxu.github.io/",
    "emails": [
      "hongtengxu@ruc.edu.cn"
    ],
    "github": "https://github.com/hongtengxu",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/hongteng-xu-85a60546/",
    "homepage_text": "Hongteng Xu\nHongteng Xu\nAssociate Professor\nGaoling School of Artificial Intelligence\nRenmin University of China\nEmail:\nhongtengxu@ruc.edu.cn\nCV\nI am an Associate Professor at\nGaoling School of Artificial Intelligence, Renmin University of China\n. From January 2018 to September 2020, I am a senior research scientist in\nInfinia ML, Inc\n. At the same time, I am a visiting researcher in\nProf. Lawrence Carin\n's research group,\nDuke University\n.\n                    From August 2017 to January 2018, I was a postdoctoral researcher in the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, Duke University, supervised by Prof. Lawrence Carin. I got my Ph.D. from the School of Electrical and Computer\n                    Engineering,\nGeorgia Tech\n. I was jointly supervised by\nProf. Hongyuan Zha\n(CSE) and\nProf. Mark A. Davenport\n(ECE).\nMy research lies in\nArtificial General Intelligence (AGI)\n. In particular, I am interested in broad topics in computational optimal transport, generative modeling, large language models, model architecture design, and their applications in AI4Math and AI4Science."
  },
