    "homepage_text": "‎\nLiangzu Peng\nHome\nPublication\nTeaching\nPhD Student\nElectrical and Systems Engineering\nInnovation in Data Engineering and Science (IDEAS)\nUniversity of Pennsylvania\nPhiladelphia, PA 19104, USA\n+1 (667) 910 4063\nlpenn at seas dot upenn dot edu\nGoogle Scholar\nOpenReview\nGitHub\nTwitter\nDBLP\nAbout Me (\nCV\n)\nI am a PhD student at UPenn. My advisor is Dr.\nRené Vidal\n.\nI work on optimization, machine learning, and computer vision.\nTopics and keywords of interest to me include\n[2022 - ]: block coordinate descent (\n1\n2\n3\n) and related algorithms (\n4\n5\n)\n[2022 - ]: lifelong/continual learning (\n1\n2\n3\n)\n[2021 - ]: geometric computer vision (\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n6\n)\nNews & Academic Activities\n[August, 2025] (Tutorial @\nCoLLAs 2025\n)\nMathematics of Continual\nLearning (based on\nthis manuscript\n)\n[November, 2024] (Talk @\nESE PhD Colloquium\n)\nPrehistory of Continual Learning and All\nElse That We Forget\n[October 1, 2024] (Preprint)\nICL-TSVD: Bridging Theory and Practice in Continual Learning with Pre-trained Models\n[June 7, 2024] (ICML 2024\nSpotlight\n, 335/9473≈3.5%)\nBlock Acceleration Without Momentum\n[April, 2024] (T-PAMI)\nACM\nand\nHERE\n[February 28, 2024] (CVPR 2024)\nScalable 3D Registration with TEAR\n[February 21, 2024] (JMLR)\nUnlabeled Principal Component Analysis and Matrix Completion\n[November 21, 2023] (\nCPAL 2024\n)\nHARD: Hyperplane ARrangement Descent\n[Summer 2023]\nInternship @ Alibaba Group, Bellevue, WA, USA\n[June 15, 2023]\nTalk @\nAI TIME\n(Virtual):\nThe Ideal Continual Learner\n[June 1, 2023] (\nMinisymposium\n@\nSIAM OP23\n, Seattle)\nFantastic Iteratively Reweighted Algorithms and Where to Find Them (\nabstract\nslides\n)\n[May 24, 2023]\nPreprint:\nBlock Coordinate Descent on Smooth Manifolds\n[April 24, 2023]\n(ICML 2023)\nThe Ideal Continual Learner: An Agent That Never Forgets\n[March 21, 2023] (CVPR 2023\nHighlight\n, 235/9155≈2.5%)\nConvergence of IRLS/GNC in Outlier-Robust Estimation\n[December 8, 2022]\nTalk @\nAI TIME\n: Robust Rotation Search: Theory and Algorithms\n[October 7, 2022]\nTop Reviewer of NeurIPS 2022\n[September 28, 2022]\nTalk @\nFlatiron Institute\n: A Tale of Two Villains (\nTheoriNet\nretreat)\n[September 14, 2022]\nNeurIPS 2022:\nConvergence rates of IRLS\n[September 9, 2022]\nTalk @\nVision Lab\n: \"RETREAT\" (internal retreat)\n[August 17, 2022]\nTalk @\nVITA\n: \"Rotation Search: Optimization Theory and Algorithm\"\n[July 26, 2022]\nTalk @\nICCOPT\n: \"Semidefinite Relaxations in Rotation Search\"\n[July 3, 2022] (ECCV 2022\nOral\n, 158/5803≈2.7%)\nSemidefinite Relaxations in Rotation Search\n[April 22, 2022]\nHighlighted Reviewer of ICLR 2022\n[March 28, 2022] (CVPR 2022\nOral\n, 342/8161≈4.2%)\nAccurate Rotation and Correspondence Search (\nARCS\n,\nslides\n)\n[Spring 2022]\nReceived\nMINDS PhD fellowship\n[September 28, 2021]\nNeurIPS 2021:\nUnlabeled Principal Component Analysis\n[July 21, 2021] (\nAppl Comput Harmon Anal\n)\nHomomorphic Sensing of Subspace Arrangements\n[May 8, 2021]\nICML 2021:\nSparse Homomorphic Sensing\n[May 1, 2021]\nISIT 2021:\nUnsigned Matrix Completion\n[March 13, 2021]\nPhD offer from Dr.\nRené Vidal\n(JHU). See\nyou soon at Baltimore."
  },
  "serper::Di Wang": {
    "homepage": "https://dw236.github.io/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": "https://github.com/kfoynt",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Di Wang\nHome\nPublication\nTalks\nTeaching\nDi Wang\nEmail:\nwangd\n[@]eecs[DOT]berkeley[DOT]edu\nAbout me\nMy research interests are in the design of efficient algorithms, especially for large-scale optimization problems and graph problems that arise broadly in applications from machine learning, data analysis and operations research. My work draws on a broad range of numerical and discrete tools from combinatorics, optimization and graph theory, which leads to not only stronger theoretical guarantees, but also practical improvements for computational challenges arising in practice.\nIn July 2019, I joined Google research at Mountain View.\nFrom Spring 2018 to Summer 2019, I was a postdoc in the Algorithms & Randomness Center at Georgia Tech with\nRichard Peng\n.\nIn Fall 2017, I was a research fellow at the\nSimons Institute\n(for the program\nBridging Continuous and Discrete Optimization\n).\nI finished my PhD in Computer Science at UC Berkeley in summer 2017, advised by\nSatish Rao\n.\nBefore attending Berkeley, I received B.S. in Computer Science from Cornell University, advised by\nRobert Kleinberg\n.\nLinks\nDan Spielman's course on spectral graph theory\nThe Design of Approximation Algorithms\nby David P. Williamson and David B. Shmoys\nStanford Network Analysis Platform (SNAP)\nNetworks, Crowds, and Markets\nby David Easley and Jon Kleinberg\nSanjeev Arora's course on learning theory\nLocal Graph Clustering\nUC Berkeley FODA (Foundations of Data Analysis) Institute"
  },
  "serper::Stephan Zheng": {
    "homepage": "https://trustmlresearch.github.io/seminar-talks/index_Stephan_Zheng.html",
    "emails": [
      "trustml.research@gmail.com"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Talk : TrustML Young Scientist Seminars\nTrustML Young Scientist Seminars\nMenu\nClose\nTalk\nEconomic Modeling, Decision-Making, and Mechanism Design using the AI Economist\nStephan Zheng\n(Salesforce Research)\nStephan Zheng (www.stephanzheng.com) is an AI researcher who has been developing the AI Economist, a multi-agent RL framework for economics. Most recently, he led a research team at Salesforce Research. Previously, he also worked on imitation learning, modeling cooperation, and robustness in deep learning and multi-agent games. His research has been published in leading machine learning conferences and scientific journals, including Science Advances, and has led to multiple patents. It has also been widely covered in US and international media, e.g., the Financial Times, MIT Tech Review, Forbes, Volkskrant, podcasts, and Dutch radio. He has served as an area chair for NeurIPS and ICML. Before machine learning, Stephan studied math and theoretical physics at Utrecht University, Harvard University, and the University of Cambridge, receiving the Lorenz graduation prize for his thesis on exotic dualities in topological string theory. He then switched to machine learning research during his PhD at Caltech. He is originally from the Netherlands.\nShort Abstract:\nSolving global socioeconomic challenges, e.g., economic inequality or sustainability, requires new tools and data-driven simulations. To this end, the AI Economist is a multi-agent reinforcement learning (RL) framework that outperforms and overcomes key limitations of traditional economics. I will survey key results in this area: 1) AI tax policies that significantly improve equality and productivity, 2) AI redistribution mechanisms that are preferred over classic ones by real people, 3) AI-powered macroeconomic simulations and boundedly rational AI agents, 4) WarpDrive, our open-source framework for multi-agent RL end-to-end on GPUs, and 5) AI for Global Climate Cooperation, a competition to design climate negotiation and agreements that incentivize global cooperation. Finally, I will survey future research directions towards real-world scale."
  },
  "serper::Yan Li": {
    "homepage": "https://gzliyan113.github.io/",
