    "homepage_text": "Yuqing Kong\nYuqing\nKong\nTenured Associate Professor\nthe Center on Frontiers of Computing Studies (CFCS)\nPeking University\nCourtyard No.5, Jing Yuan, Beijing, P.R.China, 100871\nyuqing.kong[at]pku.edu.cn\nI am currently an associate professor at the Center on Frontiers of Computing Studies (CFCS), Peking University. I obtained my Ph.D. degree from the Computer Science and Engineering Department at University of Michigan in 2018 and my bachelor degree in mathematics from University of Science and Technology of China in 2013.\nMy research interests lie in the intersection of theoretical computer science and the areas of economics: information elicitation/evaluation, prediction markets, mechanism design, and the applications of these areas to crowdsourcing and machine learning.\nPublications\n2025\nY. Guo, J. D. Hartline, Z. Huang, Y. Kong, A. Shah, F.-Y. Yu, âAlgorithmic Robust Forecast Aggregation,â in Proceedings of the ACM Conference on Economics and Computation (EC), 2025\nS. Xu, Y. Lu, G. Schoenebeck, Y. Kong, âBenchmarking LLMs' Judgments with No Gold Standardâ in Proceedings of the 13th International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), 2025\nY. Guo, Y. Kong, J. Liu, âMitigating the Participation Bias by Balancing Extreme Ratingsâ in Proceedings of the ACM Web conference (WWW), 2025, oral presentation\nY. Guo, Y. Kong, âRobust Aggregation with Adversarial Expertsâ in Proceedings of the ACM Web conference (WWW), 2025\nQ. Wang, X. Xia, Z. Yang, X. Deng, Y. Kong, Z. Zhang, L. Wang, C. Yu, J. Xu, B. Zheng âLearning against Non-credible Second-Price Auctionsâ in Proceedings of the ACM Web conference (WWW), 2025\n2024\nY. Kong, S. Wang, Y. Wang, âThe Surprising Benefits of Base Rate Neglect in Robust Aggregationâ in Proceedings of ACM Conference on Econ and Computation (EC), 2024\nY. Lu, S. Xu, Y. Zhang, Y. Kong, G. Schoenebeck, âEliciting Informative Text Evaluations with Large Language Modelsâ in Proceedings of ACM Conference on Econ and Computation (EC), 2024\nY. Kong, \"Dominantly Truthful Peer Prediction Mechanisms with a Finite Number of Tasks\" J. ACM 71, 2, Article 9 (April 2024), 49 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/3638239 Extends the work presented in SODA 2020, ITCS 2022\nY. Pan, Z. Chen, Y. Kongâ , âRobust Decision Aggregation with Second-order Informationâ in Proceedings of the ACM Web conference (WWW), 2024\n2023\nY. Kong, G. Schoenebeck, \"\nFalse Consensus, Information Theory, and Prediction Markets\n\" in Proceedings of The 13th Innovations in Theoretical Computer Science (ITCS), 2023.\nY. Lu, Y. Kong, âCalibrating 'Cheap Signals' in Peer Review without a Prior.â in Proceedings of the Thirty-seventh Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), 2023\nY. Guo, Y. Yuan, J. Zhang, Y. Kong, Z. Zhu, & Z. Cai, \"Near-optimal experimental design under the budget constraint in online platformsâ in Proceedings of the ACM Web conference (WWW), 2023\nQ. Wang, Z. Yang, X. Deng, Y. Kong, âLearning to bid in repeated first-price auctions with budgetsâ in Proceedings of the 40th International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), 2023\n2022\nY. Kong, Y. Li, Y. Zhang, Z. Huang, J. Wu, \"\nEliciting Thinking Hierarchy without a Prior\n\" in Proceedings of Thirty-sixth Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), 2022 [\nBlog\n] [\nå¬ä¼å·\n]\nZ. Huang, Y. Kong, X. Liu, G. Schoenebeck, S. Xu, \"\nBONUS! Maximizing Surprise\n\" in Proceedings of The Web Conference (WWW), 2022.\nY. Kong, â\nMore Dominantly Truthful Multi-task Peer Prediction with a Finite Number of Tasks\nâ in Proceedings of The 13th Innovations in Theoretical Computer Science (ITCS), 2022.\n2021\nZ. Huang*, S. Xu*, Y. Shan, Y. Lu, Y. Kong, X. Liu and G. Schoenebeck, \"\nSURPRISE! and When to Schedule It.\n\" in Proceedings of the 30th International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI-21)\n2020\nY. Kong, â\nDominantly Truthful Multi-task Peer Prediction with a Constant Number of Tasks\nâ in Proceedings of the ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms  (SODA), 2020. [\nTalk\n] [\nBlog\n]\nX. Sun*, Y. Xu*, P. Cao, Y. Kong, L. Hu, S. Zhang, Y. Wang, âTCGM: An Information-Theoretic Framework for Semi-Supervised Multi-Modality Learningâ in proceeding of  European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV), Oral (2%), 2020.\nY. Kong, G. Schoenebeck, B. Tao, F. Yu, âInformation Elicitation Mechanisms for Statistical Estimationâ in Proceedings of the 34th AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI), 2020.\n2019\nY. Kong, C. Peikert, G. Schoenebeck, B. Tao, â\nOutsourcing Computation: the Minimal Refereed Mechanism\nâ in Proceedings of The 15th Conference on Web and Internet Economics (WINE), 2019.\nY. Xu*, P. Cao*, Y. Kong, Y. Wang, â\nLDMI: A Novel Information-theoretic Loss Function for Training Deep Nets Robust to Label Noise\nâ in Proceedings of the Thirty-third Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), 2019. [\nBlog\n]\nP. Cao*, Y. Xu*, Y. Kong, Y. Wang, \"\nMax-MIG: an Information-Theoretic Approach for Joint Learning from Crowds\n,\" in Proceedings of the 7th International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), New Orleans, Louisiana, USA, May 6-9, 2019.\nB. Zhang*, Y. Kong*, G. Essl, E. M. Provost, \"\nf-Similarity Preservation Loss for Soft Labels: A Demonstration on Cross-Corpus Speech Emotion Recognition\n,\" in Proceedings of the 33rd AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI), 5725-5732, Honolulu, Hawaii, USA, January 27-February 1, 2019.\nY. Kong, G. Schoenebeck, \"\nAn Information-Theoretic Framework For Designing Information Elicitation Mechanisms That Reward Truth-telling\n,\" in Proceedings of the ACM Transactions on Economics and Computation (TEAC), 2:1-2:33, Volume 7 Issue 1, February 2019.\n2018\nY. Kong, G. Schoenebeck,\nâEliciting Expertise without Verificationâ\nin Proceedings of the 2018 ACM Conference on Economics and Computation (EC), 195-212. Cornell in Ithaca, New York, USA, June 18-22, 2018.\nY. Kong, G. Schoenebeck,\nâWater from Two Rocks: Maximizing the Mutual Informationâ\nin Proceedings of the 2018 ACM Conference on Economics and Computation (EC), 177-194. Cornell in Ithaca, New York, USA, June 18-22, 2018.\nY. Kong, G. Schoenebeck,\nâEquilibrium Selection in Information Elicitation without Verification via Information Monotonicityâ\nin Proceedings the 9th Innovations in Theoretical Computer Science (ITCS), 2018. Cambridge, Massachusetts, USA, January 11-14, 2018.\nY. Kong, G. Schoenebeck,\nâOptimizing Bayesian Information Revelation Strategy in Prediction Markets: the Alice Bob Alice Case â\nin Proceedings the 9th Innovations in Theoretical Computer Science (ITCS), 2018. Cambridge, Massachusetts, USA, January 11-14, 2018.\n2016\nY. Kong, K. Ligett, G. Schoenebeck,\nâPutting Peer Prediction Under the Micro(economic)scope and Making Truth-telling Focalâ\nin Proceedings of the 2016 International Conference on Web and Internet Economics (WINE), 251-264. Montreal, Canada, December 11-14, 2016.\nTutorials and Talks\nTutorial\n\"Eliciting Information without Verificationâ invited talk in\nIJTCS-FAW 2022\nâDominantly Truthful Multi-task Peer Prediction with a Constant Number of Tasksâ invited talk in\nIncentives in Machine Learning Workshop\n, ICML, 2020.\nâEliciting Information by Information Theoryâ invited talk in\nWomen in EconCS, WINE\n, 2020.\nâ\nAn Information Theoretic View of Information Elicitation Mechanisms\nâ joint organize with Grant Schoenebeck, in EC, 2017\nTeaching\nRandomness and Computation, Spring 2019, 2020, 2021, 2022, 2023\nMathematics for the Information Age, Fall 2020, 2021, 2022, 2023\nAlgorithmic Game Theory, Fall 2019\nPeople\nComing soon!"
