  "serper::Fan Yang": {
    "homepage": "https://www.fanyanggroup.com/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Home | Stanford Fan Yang Lab\ntop of page\nStanford Fan Yang Lab\nStem Cell and Biomaterials Engineering\nMission Statement\nOur lab’s mission is to develop therapies for regenerating human tissues lost due to diseases or aging, and to build tissue engineered 3D models for understanding disease progression and informing drug discovery. We invent biomaterials and engineering tools to elucidate and modulate biology, and also use biology to inform materials and engineering design. Our work is highly interdisciplinary, and is driven by unmet clinical needs or key gaps in biology. We conduct rigorous and reproducible research with translational potential, and cultivate synergistic collaborations both within and outside of our lab. We are a “people first” team, and foster a lab culture that is supportive, diverse, inclusive, friendly, fun\nBrain cancer cells form network in 3D hydrogels\nCells reorganize collagen fibers in 3D\nStem cells form protrusion in sliding hydrogels\nBrain cancer cells form network in 3D hydrogels\n1/9\nand creative! We are here to learn and help each other learn. We believe in the value of failures while taking high risk/high reward research, and support each other to keep going. We seek to positively impact the future of biotechnology and medicine via training future leaders in academia, industry, and government. Please visit\nResearch\nand\nPublications\npages to learn more.\nNews Highlights:\nThree lab members received Prestigious Fellowships!\nCongratulations to three of our lab members who recently received prestigious fellowships to support their Ph.D. work! Mark Fleck...\nNCI awards Fan Yang Lab a R01 to study Osteosarcoma!\nThe National Cancer Institute awarded our lab a 5-year R01 to harness tissue engineered 3D models for elucidating osteosarcoma biology...\nFuture of Everything Podcast Features an interview w/ Prof Yang on the Future of Tissue Engineering\nProf. Yang was recently featured by Stanford Engineering’s - The Future of Everything Podcast.  In this interview, Dr. Yang shared her...\nbottom of page"
  },
