    "homepage_text": "蘇仕揚 Shih-Yang Su\nAbout me\nHuman Motion Learning Reinforcement Learning Embodied Vision\nFollow\nVancouver, BC\nEmail\nTwitter\nLinkedIn\nGithub\n蘇仕揚 Shih-Yang Su\nI finished my Ph.D. in Computer Science at the University of British Columbia, where I worked with\nProf. Helge Rhodin\non the topics of Human Motion Learning, Character Control and Animation, and 3D Vision.\nI am fortunate to have opportunities to work with\nDr. Michael Zollhöfer\nand\nDr. Timur Bagautdinov\nat Meta Reality Labs,\nProf. Jia-Bin Huang\nat the University of Maryland, College Park,\nDr. Hossein Hajimirsadeghi\nat Borealis AI,\nProf. Chun-Yi Lee\nand\nProf. Shang-Hong Lai\nat National Tsing Hua University,\nDr. Yi-Hsuang Yang\nand\nDr. Li Su\nat Academia Sinica.\nProject pages\nNPC: Neural Point Characters from Video (ICCV 2023)\nDANBO: Disentangled Articulated Neural Body Representations (ECCV 2022)\nA-NeRF: articulated radiance fields (NeurIPS 2021)\n3D Photography (CVPR 2020)\n8 Bit converter (ICASSP 2017)\nPublications\nGaussian Shadow Casting for Neural Characters\nLuis Bolaños,\nShih-Yang Su\n, Helge Rhodin\nCVPR 2024\n[\nPaper\n] [\nProject Page (Coming soon)\n]\nMirror-Aware Neural Humans\nDaniel Ajisafe, James Tang,\nShih-Yang Su\n, Bastian Wandt, Helge Rhodin\n3DV 2024\n[\nPaper\n] [\nProject Page\n]\nNPC: Neural Point Characters from Video\nShih-Yang Su\n, Timur Bagautdinov, Helge Rhodin\nICCV 2023\n[\nPaper\n] [\nProject Page\n]\nDANBO: Disentangled Articulated Neural Body Representations via Graph Neural Networks\nShih-Yang Su\n, Timur Bagautdinov, Helge Rhodin\nECCV 2022\n[\nPaper\n] [\nProject Page\n]\nA-NeRF: Articulated Neural Radiance Fields for Learning Human Shape, Appearance, and Pose\nShih-Yang Su\n, Frank Yu, Michael Zollhöfer, Helge Rhodin\nNeurIPS 2021\n[\nPaper\n] [\nProject Page\n]\n3D Photography using context-aware layered depth inpainting\nMeng-Li Shih,\nShih-Yang Su\n, Johannes Kopf, Jia-Bin Huang\nCVPR 2020\n[\nPaper\n] [\nProject Page\n]\nThumbnail credit:\nuso_otter\nGraph generation with variational recurrent neural network\nShih-Yang Su\n, Hossein Hajimirsadeghi, Greg Mori\nNeurIPS 2019 Graph Representation Learning Workshop\n[\nPaper\n]\nDiversity-driven exploration strategy for deep reinforcement learning\nZhang-Wei Hong, Tzu-Yun Shann,\nShih-Yang Su\n, Yi-Hsiang Chang, Chun-Yi Lee\nNeurIPS 2018\n[\nPaper\n]\nVirtual-to-real: Learning to control in visual semantic segmentation\nZhang-Wei Hong, Yu-Ming Chen, Hsuan-Kung Yang,\nShih-Yang Su\n, Tzu-Yun Shann, Yi-Hsiang Chang, Brian Hsi-Lin Ho, Chih-Chieh Tu, Yueh-Chuan Chang, Tsu-Ching Hsiao, Hsin-Wei Hsiao, Sih-Pin Lai, Chun-Yi Lee\nIJCAI 2018\n[\nPaper\n]\nA deep policy inference Q-network for multi-agent systems\nShih-Yang Su\n*, Zhang-Wei Hong*, Yi-Hsiang Chang*, Tzu-Yun Shann*, and Chun-Yi Lee*\nAAMAS 2018\n[\nPaper\n]\nAutomatic conversion of pop music into chiptunes for 8-bit pixel art\nShih-Yang Su\n, Cheng-Kai Chiu, Li Su, and Yi-Hsuan Yang\nICASSP 2017\n[\nPaper\n] [\nProject Page\n]\nNews\n2024/02 - Defended my Ph.D. dissertation!\n2023/07 - Our\npoint-based human avatar\napproach is accepted to\nICCV 2023\n!\n2023/04 - I am now a Ph.D. candidate!\n2022/07 - Our\nsurface-free robust human avatar\napproach is accepted to\nECCV 2022\n!\n2021/10 - We have updated the\nA-NeRF\non arXiv to the NeurIPS version.\n2021/09 - Our\nsurface-free human body modeling\napproach is accepted to\nNeurIPS 2021\n!\n2021/03 - Submitted my reviews for ICML 2021\n2020/09 - Reviewing for ICLR 2021.\n2020/09 - Now a Ph.D. student in Computer Science at the University of British Columbia.\n2020/05 - Got my M.Sc. degree in Computer Engineering.\n2020/04 - I will serve as a reviewer for NeurIPS 2020.\n2020/02 - Our paper on\n3D Photograph\nis accepted to\nCVPR 2020\n!\n2019/10 - Our paper on\ngraph generation\nis accepted to\nNeurIPS 2019\nGRL Workshop\n!\n2019/05 - Start my internship at\nBorealis.ai\nVancouver!\n2019/04 - I will serve as a reviewer for NeurIPS 2019.\n2018/11 - I am one of the student volunteers in NeurIPS 2018. Come and say hello!\n2018/09 - Our paper on\nRL exploration strategy\nis accepted to\nNeurIPS 2018\n!\n2018/08 - Finished my military service!\n2018/04 - Start my military service.\n2018/04 - Our paper on virtual-to-real reinforcement learning is accepted to\nIJCAI 2018\n!\n2018/01 - Our paper on multi-agent learning is accepted to\nAAMAS 2018\n!"
  },
  "serper::F. Yu": {
    "homepage": "https://www.csit.carleton.ca/~fyu/",
    "emails": [
      "richard.yu@carleton.ca"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "F. Richard Yu\nF. Richard Yu\nFRSC, FCAE, MAE, FEIC, FIEEE, FIET, PEng, PhD\nProfessor, School Info. Tech. and Depart. Systems&Computer Eng.\nCarleton University\nOffice:\nCanal Building 4201\n; Email: richard.yu at carleton.ca\nProf. F. Richard Yu received the PhD degree in electrical engineering from the University of British Columbia (UBC). His research interests include intelligent and autonomous systems, machine learning and embodied AI, the Internet of things (IoT), security/privacy, and blockchain. His research has substantially influenced industry standards and design specifications. Several innovative approaches developed through his work have been implemented in commercial products and services, positively affecting millions of users worldwide.\nHe has been consistently recognized as a Clarivate âHighly Cited Researcher,â Stanford âTop 2% Most Cited Scientist,â and ScholarGPS âTop 0.05% Highly Ranked Scholarâ for several consecutive years. He has published 800+ papers in prestigious journals and conferences with 60,000+ and an H-index of 128+ (\nGoogel Scholar\n).\nHe received several Best Paper Awards from some first-tier conferences, Carleton Research Achievement Awards in 2012 and 2021, and the Ontario Early Researcher Award (formerly Premiers Research Excellence Award) in 2011.\nHe is a Member of the Academia Europaea (MAE), a Fellow of the Royal Society of Canada (FRSC), the Canadian Academy of Engineering (FCAE), Engineering Institute of Canada (FEIC), IEEE, and IET. He is a Board Member the IEEE VTS and the Editor-in-Chief for IEEE VTS Mobile World newsletter. He is a Distinguished Lecturer of the IEEE.\nResearch Rankings & Recognition\nResearch.com:\nRanked\n#8\namong the\nBest Computer Science Scientists in Canada\nRanked\n#6\namong the\nBest Electronics and Electrical Engineering Scientists in Canada\nScholarGPS.com:\nRanked\n#1\nin the\nDeep Reinforcement Learning and Mobile Edge Computing\nfields globally (Lifetime)\nRanked\n#38\namong the\nHighly Ranked Electrical and Computer Engineering Scholars\nglobally (Prior Five Years)\nRecent books:\nIntropy: A Framework for Modeling Intelligence\nA Brief History of Intelligence â The Endless Frontier\nThe Dissipative Economics Model â How Materials, Energy,\n        Information, and Intelligence Shape Markets\nChoose Colour"
  },
  "serper::Kris Cao": {
    "homepage": "http://kriscao.github.io/about/",
    "emails": [
      "cao.kris@gmail.com"
    ],
    "github": "https://github.com/kriscao",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "About | S -> NP VP\nS -> NP VP\nAbout\nI am a member of technical staff at Cohere, working on model pretraining. For my\nday job, I think about things such as tokenization, trustworthy evals, signal at\nsmall scales, model optimization, and data infrastructure. In the past, I have\nworked on topics such as (Unicode) character-based language modelling, deep\ngenerative models applied to language, and structured meaning representations.\nSelected publications\n(see my\nGoogle\nScholar\nfor a full list)\nTokenization and tokenization-free modelling\nUnpacking tokenization: Evaluating text compression and its correlation with model performance\nOmer Goldman, Avi Caciularu, Matan Eyal, Kris Cao, Idan Szpektor, Reut Tsarfaty\nWhat is the best recipe for character-level encoder-only modelling?\nKris Cao\nYou should evaluate your language model on marginal likelihood over tokenizations\nKris Cao, Laura Rimell\nA joint model of word embedding and word morphology\nKris Cao, Marek Rei\nGeneral language modelling\nCommand A: An Enterprise-Ready Large Language Model\nCohere\nGemini 1.5: Unlocking multimodal understanding across millions of tokens of context\nGemini Team\nGemini: A Family of Highly Capable Multimodal Models\nGemini Team\nPitfalls of Static Language Modelling\nAngeliki Lazaridou, Adhiguna Kuncoro, Elena Gribovskaya, Devang Agrawal, Adam\nLiska, Tayfun Terzi, Mai Gimenez, Cyprien de Masson d’Autume, Sebastian Ruder,\nDani Yogatama, Kris Cao, Tomas Kocisky, Susannah Young, Phil Blunsom\nMiscellaneous\nControl prefixes for parameter-efficient text generation\nJordan Clive, Kris Cao, Marek Rei\nTowards coherent and consistent use of entities in narrative generation\nPinelopi Papalampidi, Kris Cao, Tomas Kocisky\nGame Plan: What AI can do for Football, and What Football can do for AI\nKarl Tuyls, Shayegan Omidshafiei, Paul Muller, Zhe Wang, Jerome Connor, Daniel\nHennes, Ian Graham, William Spearman, Tim Waskett, Dafydd Steele, Pauline Luc,\nAdria Recasens, Alexandre Galashov, Gregory Thornton, Romuald Elie, Pablo\nSprechmann, Pol Moreno, Kris Cao, Marta Garnelo, Praneet Dutta, Michal Valko,\nNicolas Heess, Alex Bridgland, Julien Perolat, Bart De Vylder, Ali Eslami,\nMark Rowland, Andrew Jaegle, Remi Munos, Trevor Back, Razia Ahamed, Simon\nBouton, Nathalie Beauguerlange, Jackson Broshear, Thore Graepel, Demis Hassabis\nFactorising AMR generation through syntax\nKris Cao, Stephen Clark\nEmergent communication through negotiation\nKris Cao, Angeliki Lazaridou, Marc Lanctot, Joel Z Leibo, Karl Tuyls, Stephen\nClark"
  },
  "serper::Jason Y. Zhang": {
