    "homepage_text": "Namhoon Lee\nNamhoon Lee\nnamhoon.lee [at] postech.ac.kr\nI am an Associate Professor of\nCSE\nand\nAI\nat\nPOSTECH\nwhere I lead the\nComputational Optimization Lab\n.\n            Previously, I received my PhD from\nOxford University\n.\nI am interested in optimization for large-scale machine learning.\nProspective students:\nPlease check the\nhiring page\nfor more information.\nNews\nMar 2026\nSelected for the \"Early-Career Researcher Infrastructure Support\" Program (Advanced Infrastructure Track).\n            We have secured 500 million KRW to establish cutting-edge research facilities, fostering global research excellence and expanding shared infrastructure opportunities."
  },
  "serper::Yaofeng Desmond Zhong": {
    "homepage": "https://desmondzhong.com/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": "https://github.com/DesmondZhong",
    "linkedin": "//www.linkedin.com/in/desmondzhong/",
    "homepage_text": "Yaofeng Desmond Zhong - Home\nHello, I’m\nYaofeng \"Desmond\" Zhong\nFounding Research Scientist @ Breakpoint AI\nAbout me\nI'm Yaofeng \"Desmond\" Zhong, a founding research scientist at Breakpoint AI. Previous, I was a research scientist in the physics-informed AI research group at Siemens Technology. I'm broadly interested in computer vision and scientific machine learning. In particular, my research interest lies in the intersection of\ndeep learning\n,\ndynamical systems\nand\ncontrol\n. I've been working on incorporating physics domain knowledge into machine learning as well as differentiable simulation for optimal control. I also make relevant research code public since I believe the research community thrives through open-source. At Siemens, I've contributed to the research and development of\nSIMATIC Robot Pick AI\n, an AI-based computer vision software that enables robots for intelligent bin picking.\nI received my Ph.D. from Princeton University, advised by\nProf. Naomi Leonard\n. My dissertation investigates cascade dynamics, decision making and physics-constrained machine learning. While at Princeton, I co-initiated the Robotics Reading Group. Prior to Princeton, I received my B.Eng. from Tsinghua University. My undergraduate thesis is on adaptive remeshing in finite element method, supervised by\nProf. Hongzhi Zhong\n. While at Tsinghua, I led a team to participate in COMAP's international\nMathematical Contest in Modeling (MCM)\nand my team was designated as\nOutstanding Winner\n.\nProduction Projects\nSIMATIC Robot Pick AI @ Siemens\nOur AI-driven vision software enables robots to perform previously manual-only tasks such as picking unknown objects, empowering our industrial partners to unlock unprecedented levels of efficiency and productivity.\nProduct Page\nPrototype Projects\nTowards Industrial Metaverse @ Siemens\nWe developed an early prototype of a surrogate machine learning model for computational fluid dynamics (CFD) simulation. We designed and implemented surrogate models for predicting air flow around a race car (SimRod). The air flow computed from the surrogate model is visualized along with the air flow computed from Siemens Simcenter STAR-CCM+. The whole 3D scene is assembled in NVIDIA Omniverse.\nResearch Projects\nImproving Gradient Computation\nDifferentiable simulation enables gradients to be back-propagated through physics simulations. Then we can learn the dynamics and properties of a physics system by gradient-based optimization. However, differentiable simulation at its current stage might provide wrong gradients that deteriorate its performance in learning tasks, especially when contact and collision exist. In this work, we propose to improve gradient computation by continuous collision detection.\nBlog\nL4DC 2023\narXiv\nStart animation\nStop animation\nReset animation\nFast\nNormal\nSlow\nBenchmark: Differentiable Simulators\nMany open-source differentiable physics simulators appear in recent years: diffTaichi, Google Brax, Nvidia Warp, Stanford Nimble, DiffCoSim, etc. Do the calculated gradients agree with one another? We find that they don't agree, which affects their performance on optimiazation and learning tasks.\nICML 2022 (AI4Science)\narXiv\nEMVLight\nEmergency vehicles (EMVs) play a crucial role in responding to time-critical events. To reduce the travel time of EMVs, techniques of route optimization and traffic signal control has been studied as two separate modules. However, the routing problem and the traffic signal control problem are coupled and traditional solution usually increases the travel time of other vehicles significantly. Thus, we propose EMVLight, a decentralized reinforcement learning framework, to tackle these two problems at the same time.\nAAAI 2022\narXiv\nDifferentiable Contact Models\nOne of the limitation of Lagrangian/Hamiltonian neural networks is that they cannot handle dynamical systems with discontinuity. A common type of discontinuity in physical systems is collision and contact. In order to model collision and contact, we extend Lagrangian/Hamiltonian neural networks with a differentiable contact model. The proposed simulation pipeline is end-to-end differentiable, so that we can simutaneously learn unknown mass, energy, coefficient of friction and restitution. The learned dynamics can also be used as a differentiable physics simulator for downstream gradient-based optimization tasks.\nNeurIPS 2021\narXiv\nMulti-Robot Task Allocation\nWe design a decision-making algorithm that defines how the robots select tasks to perform and how they repeatedly revise their task selections in response to changes in the environment. Through experiments with a multi-robot trash collection application, we assess the algorithm’s responsiveness to changing environments and resilience to failure of individual robots.\nICRA 2021\narXiv\nLearning Dynamics from Images\nHow to learn interpretable dynamics from image sequences? As an attempt to answer this question, we introduce an unsupervised neural network model that learns Lagrangian dynamics from images, with interpretability that benefits prediction and control. Our proposed model infers Lagrangian dynamics on generalized coordinates that are simultaneously learned with a coordinate-aware variational autoencoder (VAE).\nNeurIPS 2020\narXiv\nSymplectic ODE-Net\nHow to incorporate physics domain knowledge into deep learning so that the learned physics model obeys the physics law? In this work, we propose Symplectic ODE-Net to learn Hamiltonian dynamics with control by designing the computation graph. In this way, we can use less data to train a model that is interpretable and generalizes better.\nICLR 2020\narXiv"
  },
  "serper::Hui Xiong": {
    "homepage": "https://hkustai.github.io/",
    "emails": [
      "xionghui@ust.hk"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Home Page for Prof. Hui Xiong\nProf. Hui Xiong\nAssociate Vice-President\nOffice of Knowledge\n                Transfer\nChair Professor\nAI Thrust, HKUST\n                (Guangzhou)\nAIS, HKUST (Clear Water\n                Bay)\nDirector\nAI+ Lab\nEmail: xionghui@ust.hk\nOffice: C1 W 201\nHui Xiong is a Chair Professor, Associate Vice President (Knowledge Transfer), and Founding Head of the AI\n        Thrust at\nHong Kong University of Science\n          and Technology\n          (Guangzhou)\n.\n        His research interests span Artificial Intelligence, data mining, and mobile computing. He obtained his PhD in\n        Computer Science from the\nUniversity of Minnesota\n,\n        USA. Before joining\n        HKUST(GZ), he was a Distinguished Professor at\nRutgers University\nand held the\n        position of Chief\n        Scientist (Smart City) and Deputy Dean at\nBaidu Research Institute\n,\n        overseeing five research\n        labs.\nDr. Xiong has served on numerous organization and program committees for conferences, including as the Founding\n        Editor-in-Chief of\nNature npj\n          - Artificial intelligence\n, a\n        co-Editor in-Chief of the\nEncyclopedia of GIS (Springer)\n, Program Co-Chair for the\n        Industrial and Government Track\n        for the 18th\nACM\n          SIGKDD International\n          Conference on Knowledge\n          Discovery and Data Mining (KDD)\n, Program Co-Chair for the\nIEEE 2013 International Conference on Data Mining (ICDM)\n,\n        General Co-Chair for the\n2015 IEEE International\n          Conference on Data Mining\n          (ICDM)\n, and Program Co-Chair of the Research Track for the\n2018 ACM SIGKDD\n          International Conference\n          on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining\n.\nHe has received numerous prestigious honors, including election as a\n        Fellow of the\nACM\nin 2026,\nCCF\nand\nAAAI\nin 2025,\nCAAI\nin 2024,\n        and both the\nAAAS\nand the\nIEEE\nin 2020.\n\n        His awards include the 2021 AAAI Best Paper Award, the 2011 IEEE ICDM Best Research Paper Award,\n        the 2018 Ram Charan Management Practice Award – Grand Prize from the Harvard Business Review,\n        and the Gold Medal together with the Swiss Automobile Club Prize at the 2025 International Exhibition of\n        Inventions of Geneva.\nQuick Links:\nFor more details of my work, please see\nGoogle Scholar\n.\nIf you are interested in working in my research group, please read\nFor Prospective Students\n.\nNEWS\nJan. 2026\nProf. Xiong elected ACM Fellow\nRecognized by the Association for Computing Machinery for outstanding contributions to data mining and AI.\nSource\nDec. 2025\nProf. Xiong elected CCF Fellow\nThe China Computer Federation honored Prof. Hui Xiong as a 2025 CCF Fellow for contributions to data mining\n          and intelligent systems.\nSource\nNov. 2025\nProf. Xiong appointed Counselor to the Guangzhou Municipal Government\nThe Guangzhou Municipal People’s Government appointed Prof. Hui Xiong as a Counselor.\nSource\nSept. 2025\nProf. Xiong elected Vice President of the 7th Council of the Guangzhou Western Returned Scholars\n            Association\nThe first member congress and 110th anniversary celebration were held at the Dongfang Hotel, where Prof. Hui\n          Xiong was elected as Vice President of the 7th Council.\nSource\nSept. 2025\nProf. Xiong named among the World’s Top 2% Scientists 2025 by Stanford University\nProf. Hui Xiong was recognized on Stanford University’s 2025 list of the World’s Top 2% Scientists for his\n          outstanding research impact.\nSource\nSept. 2025\nProf. Xiong attended the 80th Anniversary Victory Day Ceremony in Beijing\nInvited to Tiananmen Square, Prof. Hui Xiong joined national leaders and guests to commemorate the 80th\n          anniversary of victory in the War of Resistance Against Japanese Aggression.\nSource\nMay 2025\nProf. Xiong delivered a keynote at the Guangzhou AI Forum\nHe presented expert insights on AI development and industry integration at the Guangzhou Theoretical Learning\n          Group Session and Learning Forum, attended by top city officials.\nSource\nMay. 2025\nProf. Xiong visited Ambassadors of Singapore and the UAE\nIn Beijing, he met with H.E.\nPeter Tan\nand H.E.\nHussain Al Hammadi\nto discuss AI collaboration\n          and knowledge transfer.\nSource\nMay 2025\nProf. Xiong awarded Guangdong Provincial Model Worker honor\nAt the 2025 May Day celebration in Guangzhou, Prof. Xiong was recognized as a Model Worker in Guangdong\n          Province.\nSource\nApr. 2025\nProf. Xiong received the Gold Medal and the Swiss Automobile Club Prize\nAt the 2025 International Exhibition of Inventions of Geneva, he was honored with these top recognitions.\nSource\nJan. 2025\nProf. Xiong elected as AAAI Fellow\nRecognized for his outstanding contributions to the field of artificial intelligence.\nSource\nGROUP\nI am extremely fortunate to work with a number of excellent students and postdocs.\nYou can learn more about the group\nhere\n.\nTEACHING\nHKUST (Guangzhou)\nFoundations of Data Mining (AI 5030), Fall 2023\nCross-disciplinary Research Methods, Fall 2023\nCross-disciplinary Research Methods, Spring 2023\nFoundations of Data Mining (AI 5030), Fall 2022\nTeaching Evaluation: 4.9 out of 5\nHKUST DBA\nData Mining and Business Intelligence (22:135:703), Spring 2021\nRutgers University\nData Mining (26:198:644) (Graduate Course):\nFall 2006, Spring 2009, Spring 2011, Fall 2011, Fall 2012, Fall 2013, Fall 2014, Spring 2015,\nFall 2015, Spring 2016, Fall 2016, Spring 2017, Fall 2017, Fall 2020, Spring 2021\nInformation Technology for Managers (22:198:504) (Full-time MBA):\nSpring 2008, Spring 2010, Spring 2011\nComputers & Information Systems (22:198:604) (Part-time MBA):\nSpring 2007, Fall 2008\nComputer Network Applications (29:623:375) (Undergraduate):\nFall 2005, Fall 2007, Fall 2008, Spring 2006, Spring 2007, Spring 2010\nData Mining for Business Intelligence (Undergraduate): Fall 2010\nManagement Information Systems (29:623:220) (Undergraduate):\nFall 2005, Spring 2006, Fall 2007\nSERVICE\nHonors and Distinctions\nAAAI Fellow\nCCF Fellow\nAAAS Fellow\nIEEE Fellow\nCAAI Fellow\nACM Fellow\nProfessional Services\nCCF Task Force on Big Data, Deputy Director, September 2023 - Present\nSecretary/Executive Committee, ACM SIGKDD, Present\nThe 2023 IEEE CS Fellow Evaluating Committee\nThe 2021 IEEE CS Fellow Evaluating Committee\nJournal Editorship\nEditor-in-Chief,\nnpj - Artificial Intelligence\n, 2024 - Present\nAssociate Editor,\nIEEE TKDE\n, 2013 - 2018\nAssociate Editor,\nDKE\n, 2018 - Present\nAssociate Editor,\nACM TMIS\n, 2015 - Present\nAssociate Editor,\nACM TKDD\n, 2015 - Present\nAssociate Editor,\nIEEE TBD\n, 2015 - 2020\nAssociate Editor,\nKAIS (Springer)\n, 2007 - Present\nEditorial Board Member,\nJournal of Big Data (Springer)\n, 2014 - Present\nSpecial Issue Editor,\nACM TIST\n, Intelligent Mobile Knowledge Discovery\nSpecial Issue Editor,\nWWW Journal\n, Web Information Management in the Big Data Era, 2013\nConference Service\nArea Chair, ICML 2026\nChair, Test of Time Award, WSDM 2021\nChair, IEEE ICDM Awards Committee, 2020\nGeneral Co-Chair, IEEE DSC 2020\nInvited Talk Chair, KDD 2019\nProgram Co-Chair, KDD 2018\nVice Chair, IEEE ICDM 2017\nProgram Co-Chair, DASFAA 2016\nGeneral Co-Chair, IEEE ICDM 2015\nTutorial Co-Chair, IEEE ICDM 2014\nSponsorship Chair, SDM 2014\nProgram Co-Chair, IEEE ICDM 2013\nPC Chair, WAIM 2013\nDemo Chair, KDD 2013\nIndustry Track Co-Chair, KDD 2012\nPC Vice Chair, IEEE ICDM 2012\nGeneral Chair, NDBC China 2012\nTutorial Chair, WI 2012\nFinance Chair, ICDE 2012\nPC Chair, KSEM 2011\nDemo Chair, KDD 2011\nPC Vice Chair, WI 2011\nTutorial Chair, PAKDD 2011\nPC Vice Chair, IEEE ICDM 2010\nSession Chair, KDD 2010\nPC Chair, SKM 2010\nPC Chair, NDC 2010\nPC Vice Chair, WI 2010\nPublicity Chair, NASA CIDU 2010\nPublication Chair, SDM 2010\nSession Chair, KDD 2009\nSession Chair, INFORMS 2009\nPublicity Chair, KDD 2009\nCo-Chair, SADM@ICDM 2009\nSession Chair, KDD 2008\nCo-Chair, SADM@ICDM 2008\nTrack Chair, AMIGE 2008\nSession Chair, ICDM 2007\nSession Chair, KDD 2007\nSession Chair, SDM 2007\nPublicity Chair, SDM 2007\nAwards Chair, ICMLA 2007\nSession Chair, SDM 2006\nPC Vice Chair, ICTAI 2006\nPublicity Chair, ICDM 2006\nTutorial Chair, ICMLA 2006\nPublicity Chair, WI/IAT 2006\nPC Vice Chair, PRICAI 2006\nMEDIA HIGHLIGHTS\nBelow are selected media interviews and features.\n2020\nInterviewed and Featured by\nScientia\nin the article:\n\"Harnessing Big Data to Identify Ideal Locations for Warehouses and Bike Share Stations\"\nSource\n2016\nInterviewed and Featured by\nThe Economist\nin the article:\n\"Cutpurse capers\"\nSource\n2014\nInterviewed and Quoted by\nForbes\nin the article:\n\"The Ethics of Big Data\"\nSource\n2010\nInterviewed and Quoted by\nStar Ledger\nregarding Rutgers Information Systems\nSource"
  },
  "serper::Z. Xu": {
    "homepage": "https://sites.google.com/view/xuzhouli",
    "emails": [
      "xuzhouli13561261256@ucla.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Zhouli Xu 徐宙利\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nZhouli Xu 徐宙利\nZhouli Xu 徐宙利\nAddress: UCLA Department of Mathematics,\n520 Portola Plaza\n, L\nos Angeles\n, CA\n90095-1555\nOffice:\nMS\n6931\nemail:\nxuzhouli12356125661@\nucla.edu\n(delete all numbers)\nI am a Professor\nin the\nDepartment of Mathematics\nat UCLA\n,\nsince\nNovember\n2024.\nFrom 2020 to 2024, I held the positions of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor with tenure, and Professor in the\nDepartment of Mathematics\nat\nUC San Diego\n.\nPrior to that, from 2017 to 2020, I was a C.L.E. Moore Instructor in the\nDepartment of Mathematics\nat\nMassachusetts Institute of Technology\n,\nunder the mentorship of\nHaynes Miller\n.\nI received my Ph. D. from\nthe University of Chicago\nin 2017,\nmentored by\nJ. Peter May\n,\nDaniel C. Isaksen\n, and\nMark Mahowald\n.\nHere is my\nCV\n.\nI\nteach Math 110BH in the winter quarter\n. See C\nanvas for more info.\nOn the Last Kervaire Invariant Problem\n(\narXiv\n)\nTheorem\n(\nLin\n--\nWang\n--Xu) The element $h_6^2$ survives to the $E_\\infty$-page in the Adams spectral sequence.\nCorollary\nThere exist framed manifolds of Kervaire invariant one in dimension 126.\nTheorem\n(Kervaire--Milnor 63', Browder 69', Mahowald--Tangora 67',\nBarratt--Jones--Mahowald 84', Hill--Hopkins--Ravenel 16', Lin--Wang--Xu 24'\n)\nSmooth framed manifolds with\nKervaire invariant\none exist in and only in dimensions 2, 6, 14, 30, 62, and 126.\nAdams charts\non Weinan's website.\nMinicourse\ngiven at\neCHT\n.\nA\nQuanta Magazine Article\nby\nErica Klarreich\non our work.\nA\nMath Plus Magazine Article\nby\nMarianne Freiberger\n, and a\npodcast\nfor an interview at the Isaac Newton Institute.\nAn\nEpsiloon Magazine Article\nby Noé Bente on our work.\nI'm an\nEditor\nof\nMathematische Zeitschrift\n. Submissions of papers in\ntopology are welcome!\nI'm an\nAssociate Editor\nof\nPeking Mathematical Journal\n. Submissions of papers in topology are welcome!\nI'm an\nEditor\nof\nCommunications in Analysis and Geometry\n. Submissions of papers in topology are welcome!\nI am deeply honored to have been\nnamed 2025-26\nAMS Centennial Research Fellow\n.\nI\nam deeply\nhonored to have been\nnamed as a\nFellow of the American Mathematical Society\nof class 2023.\nI\nam deeply\nhonored to receive the\nK-Theory Prize\nin 2022.\nI am deeply honored to speak at the\nICM 2022\n.\nHere is a\nlink of my ICM talk\nwith\nGuozhen Wan\ng.\nMy research was partially sponsored by\nNSF Grant DMS 2105462 (2021-2024)\n,\nand\nNSF Grant DMS 1810638 (2018-2020)\n, and\nDMS 2043485 (2020-2021)\n.\nMy papers are on my\nresearch page\n. You may also find the videos of some of my talks on this page.\nHere are\nsome Adams charts\nof the computations of the classical and C-motivic stable homotopy groups of spheres that I have done with\nDaniel C. Isaksen\nand\nGuozhen Wang\n.\nUCLA Topology Seminar\nUCSD Topology Seminar\nM.I.T. Topology Seminar\nUChicago Topology Seminar\nResearch Interests:\nAlgebraic Topology -- more specifically, classical, motivic and equivariant stable homotopy groups of spheres, with connections and applications to algebraic geometry and geometric topology.\nMy collaborators:\nTom Bachmann\n,\nAgnès Beaudry\n,\nMark Behrens\n,\nEva Belmont\n,\nPrasit Bhattacharya\n,\nIrina Bobkova\n,\nRobert Burklund\n,\nHood Chatham\n,\nDominic Culver\n,\nBogdan Gheorghe\n,\nMichael Hill\n,\nMichael Hopkins\n, Yueshi Hou,\nDaniel C. Isaksen\n,\nHana Jia Kong\n,\nManuel Krannich\n,\nAchim Krause\n,\nGuchuan Li\n,\nJianfeng Lin\n,\nWeinan Lin\n,\nYutao Liu\n,\nYunze Lu\n, Viet-Cuong Pham,\nDouglas Ravenel\n,\nXiaoLin Danny Shi\n,\nMatthew Stoffregen\n,\nGuozhen Wang\n, Yuchen Wu,\nShangjie Zhang\n,\nYifei Zhu\nConferences I co-organized:\nComputations in stable homotopy theory\n. American Institute of Mathematics, Pasadena, California. October 2025.\nICMS workshop: Young homotopy theorist meeting\n. ICMS, Bayes Centre, Edinburgh, UK.\nOctober 2025.\nWorkshop on Theory and Computation in Mathematical Physics and Topology\n.\nTianyuan Mathematics Research Center, Kunming, China. September 202\n5\n.\nSpecial Session on Chromatic Homotopy Theory\n, Fall 202\n5\nSectional Meeting of the AMS Western Section.\nUniversity of Denver, Denver, CO. August 2025.\nIWoAT 2025\n. Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, China, July 2025.\nSummer school on computations with the Adams spectral sequence and the Last Kervaire Invariant Problem\n. Westlake University, Hangzhou, China, July 2025.\nWorks\nhop on Stable Homotopy Theory\n. TSIMF. Sanya, China, March 2025.\nIWoAT Special Conference in honor of Prof. J. Peter May.\nSUSTech, Shenzhen, China,\nMarch\n20\n25\n.\nIWoAT Symposium at SUSTech,\nShenzhen, China,\nNovember 2024\n.\nComputer-assisted research in geometry and topology\nTianyuan Mathematics Research Center, Kunming, China. September 2024.\nInternational Workshop on Algebraic Topology 2024\n.\nShanghai Center for Mathematical Sciences (SCMS), Shanghai, China, Ju\nly\n2024.\nIWoAT Summer School 2024: Motivic Stable Homotopy Theory\nShanghai Center for Mathematical Sciences (SCMS), Shanghai, China, June 2024.\nSpecial Session on Homotopy theory\n, Fall 2023 Sectional Meeting of the AMS Western Section. Creighton University, Omaha, NE.\nIWoAT Summer School 2023 on\n“Operads, spectra, and multiplicative structures”\n, Yanqi Lake Beijing Institute of Mathematical Sciences and Applications, Beijing, China, August 14-19, 2023.\nInternational Workshop on Algebraic Topology 2023\n(IWoAT 2023),  Beijing International Center for Mathematical Research (BICMR),\nBeijing, China, July 24-28, 2023.\nSummer School on Chromatic Homotopy Theory and Higher (Infinity-Categorical) Algebra\n, Shanghai Center for Mathematical Sciences & School of Mathematical Sciences, Fudan University Yanqi Lake Beijing Institute of Mathematical Sciences and Applications, August 15-26, 2022.\nSummer School on Equivariant Homotopy Theory\n, Shanghai Center for Mathematical Sciences (SCMS), Shanghai, China, July 2021.\nSummer School on Equivariant Homotopy Theory\n, Shanghai Center for Mathematical Sciences (SCMS), Shanghai, China, August 2019.\nInternational Workshop on Algebraic Topology 2019\n(IWoAT 2019), Shanghai Center for Mathematical Sciences (SCMS), Shanghai, China, August 2019.\nInternational Workshop on Algebraic Topology 2018\n(IWoAT 2018), SUSTech, Shenzhen, China, June 2018.\nMidwest Topology Seminar\n, University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois, May 2017.\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse"
  },
