  "serper::Yizhou Jiang": {
    "homepage": "https://faculty.erau.edu/Yizhou.Jiang",
    "emails": [
      "yizhou.jiang@erau.edu"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "*\nWe use cookies to enhance your browsing experience, personalize content and analyze our traffic.\nBy clicking “OK,” you consent to our use of cookies.\nPrivacy Statement & GDPR\nOK\nSkip to main content\nEmbry-Riddle Home\n×\nMenu\nFind by Name\nFind by College\nFind by Department\nMenu\nHomepage\nFind Faculty By:\nName\nCollege\nDepartment\nYizhou Jiang\nTitle\nAssistant Professor\nEmail\nYizhou.Jiang@erau.edu\nEmail\nDepartment\nAerospace Engineering Department\nCollege\nCollege of Engineering\nExternal Links\nThe views expressed on faculty and external web pages are those of the author and do not necessarily represent those of Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University.\nGoogle Scholar\nBiography\nDr. Yizhou Jiang earned his Ph.D. in Industrial Engineering and Operations Research, along with an M.S. in Electrical Engineering, from the University of Illinois Chicago. Before joining ERAU, he served as a Postdoctoral Research Associate at the Center for Advanced Manufacturing at the University of Southern California.\nThe mission of the Jiang Lab at ERAU is to advance the frontiers of science and technology at the intersection of materials and manufacturing to address grand challenges in structural design and engineering. Our research integrates expertise in mechanics, materials science, and advanced manufacturing to foster innovation across multiple scales and environments. Current efforts focus on innovative space-based manufacturing and design, functional material development for next-generation manufacturing, and analytical and numerical methods for modeling, understanding, and optimizing manufacturing processes.\nEducation\nPh.D. - Doctor of Philosophy in Industrial Engineering and Operations Research,\nUniversity of Illinois at Chicago\nM.S. - Master of Science in Electrical and Computer Engineering,\nUniversity of Illinois at Chicago\nCurrently Teaching\nAE 316\n:\nA/Space Engineering Materials\nAERO 316\n:\nA/Space Engineering Materials\nAE 316HON\n:\nA/Space Engineering Materials\nAE 800\n:\nDissertation\nAE 700\n:\nThesis\nAwards, Honors and Recognitions\n2021 ASME MSEC Best Doctoral Symposium Award\n2023 Faculty Innovation Research in Science and Technology Award\n2025 Tej Gupta Outstanding Teacher Award Finalist\n2025 Research Innovation Award"
  },
  "serper::Shengpu Tang": {
    "homepage": "https://shengpu-tang.me/",
    "emails": [
      "shengpu.tang@gmail.com"
    ],
    "github": "https://github.com/shengpu-tang",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/shengpu-tang",
    "homepage_text": "Shengpu Tang\nShengpu Tang\n唐笙普\nAssistant Professor\nCS @ Emory\nPlease check out the\nLab\nsections above for info about my research group:\nTang's AI Lab\n.\nUpdate Spring 2026\n: Students who are interested in working on a research project with me, please read this first:\nReaching Out\n.\nI am a tenure-track assistant professor of computer science at Emory University. My research focuses on developing and applying\nartificial intelligence\nmethods to enhance\ndecision making in healthcare\n, with a particular emphasis on\nreinforcement learning\n. Inspired by specific real-world use cases, my work identifies technical challenges and proposes novel solutions that are broadly relevant to fundamental AI/ML research. My past contributions span various technical areas including reinforcement learning (\nRLC'25 workshops\n,\nNeurIPS'23\n,\nICML'23 workshops\n,\nNeurIPS'22 oral\n,\nNeurIPS'22 workshop\n,\nMLHC'21\n,\nICML'20\n), time-series and sequence data modeling (\nMLHC'19\n), dataset bias (\nHealthAffairs'21\n), as well as translating AI solutions to advance precision medicine (\nJMIR Med Inform'25\n,\nJAMA Network Open'25\n,\nIDWeek'24\n,\nAJS'21\n,\nJAMIA'20\n,\nJCO-CCI'20\n), with some work specifically addressing the COVID-19 pandemic (\nBMJ'22\n,\nAnnalsATS'20\n). I hope to apply my knowledge and expertise to interdisciplinary problems in healthcare and beyond.\nI served as a main organizer for\nMLHC\n2025 and\nML4H\n2022-2023, Senior AC for\nCHIL\n2025, and AC for\nCHIL\n2024. I also regularly review for top-tier ML venues, including NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, AAAI, AISTATS, KDD, MLHC, CHIL, TMLR, TKDD.\nI am a\n\"Triple Wolverine\"\n- having done my bachelor's, master's, and PhD degrees all at\nUniversity of Michigan\n. During my PhD, I was a member of the Machine Learning for Data-Driven Decisions (\nMLD3\n) research group led by Prof.\nJenna Wiens\n, under the\nMichigan AI Lab\n. I also collaborated with Prof.\nMaggie Makar\nat UMich, Prof.\nSonali Parbhoo\nat Imperial, and Prof.\nFinale Doshi-Velez\nat Harvard.\nInterests\nReinforcement Learning\nMachine Learning\nArtificial Intelligence\nHealth & Medicine\nEducation\nPhD in Computer Science & Engineering, 2024\nUniversity of Michigan, Ann Arbor\nMSE in Computer Science & Engineering, 2020\nUniversity of Michigan, Ann Arbor\nBSE in Computer Science, Minor in Mathematics,\nsumma cum laude\n, 2018\nUniversity of Michigan, Ann Arbor\nRecent News\nOct 2025\n: Congratulations to Yingchuan Sun on the acceptance of his\nML4H proceedings paper!\nJun 2025\n: 4 papers accepted at RLC 2025 workshops! Congratulations to students\nSixing\n,\nYingchuan\n,\nRolf and Matthew\n!\nFeb 2025\n: Presented at AAAI-25 New Faculty Highlights, Philadelphia, PA.\n[Recording]\nOct 2024\n: Gave an invited talk and also presented an oral abstract at IDWeek 2024 in Los Angeles, CA.\nSept 2024\n: My PhD dissertation is available online at\nthis link\n!\nAug 2024\n: Gave an invited talk at RLC 2024 ICBINB workshop, Amherst, MA.\nJune 3, 2024\n: 🎉 I defended my PhD thesis! Special thanks to my thesis committee for their valuable feedback and all my reserach collaborators who have spent time with me throughout my PhD! You can find the recordings at the following links:\nthe Preamble\nand\nthe Defense\n.\nDec 2023\n: 🎉 I presented my latest work on\nCounterfactual Annotations and Semi-Offline Evaluation\nat\nNeurIPS 2023\nin New Orleans, LA. I was also given a travel award at NeurIPS 2023!\nDec 2023\n: 🙌 We successfully organized the\nML4H 2023 Symposium\n, co-located with NeurIPS 2023 in New Orleans, LA. Kudos to the entire organizing team!\nJuly 2023\n: I attended\nICML 2023\nin Honolulu, Hawaii, where I presented\nFactored Actions for OPE\nat the Counterfactuals workshop and the Frontiers4LCD workshop. I also participated in the surfing social!\nJune 2023\n: I attended the\nCHIL 2023 Doctoral Symposium\nin Cambridge, MA.\nDec 2022\n: 🏆 I presented our paper on\nFactored Action Spaces for Offline RL in Healthcare\nat\nNeurIPS 2022\nin New Orleans, LA. It was also selected as \"\nOral\n\" and was presented during the NeurIPS 2022 virtual week.\nDec 2022\n: 🎉 I presented my internship project\nTowards Data-Driven Offline Simulations of Online RL\nat the\n3rd Offline RL workshop\nat NeurIPS 2022.\nNov 2022\n: 🙌 We successfully organized the\nML4H 2022 Symposium\n, co-located with NeurIPS 2022 in New Orleans, LA. Kudos to the entire organizing team!\nOct 2022\n: 🥈 I was extremely honored to have been selected as the\nfinalist\nrepresenting the AI lab, and\nplacing second\nin the\n19th annual CSE Graduate Honors Competition\n.\nJul-Aug 2022\n: Finally attended my\nfirst\nin-person\nconferences\nduring PhD,\nICML 2022\nin Baltimore, MD and\nMLHC 2022\nin Durham, NC.\nJuly 2022\n: I won a free registration to\nICML 2022\nas one of the top reviewers!\nMay-Aug 2022\n: I worked with Microsoft's\nAzure Cognitive Services\nteam as a Research Intern.\nFebruary 2022\n: Our paper on\nM-CURES\n, an open-source machine learning model\npredicting deterioration of hospitalized COVID-19 patients\nwas accepted to one of the world's leading medical journals,\nThe BMJ\n!\nDecember 2021\n: Our paper on\nrespecting autonomy & enabling diversity\nfor medical databanks that drive precision health and clinical AI - was published in\nHealth Affairs\n.\nDecember 2021\n: I co-organized a new workshop -\nResearch2Clinics -- Bridging the Gap: From Machine Learning Research to Clinical Practice\n- at\nNeurIPS 2021\n. Thank you to everyone who contributed and attended!\nAugust 2021\n: Invited to give a talk on\nRL with Set-Valued Policy: A Framework for Clinician-in-the-Loop Decision Making\nat the\nPathCheck Global Health Innovators Talk Series\n! You can find the recording of the seminar\nhere\n.\nJuly-August 2021\n: I presented our paper\nPractical Considerations for Offline RL Model Selection\nat both\nMLHC 2021\nand\nRL4RealLife @ ICML 2021\n! Watch the spotlight talk\nhere\n.\nDecember 2020\n: Won a free registration to\nNeurIPS 2020\nas one of the top reviewers! Excited to see everyone there :)\nJuly 2020\n: I presented our paper on\nRL with Set-Valued Policy\nat\nICML 2021\n. Check out the spotlight video\nhere\n.\nMedia Coverage\nJun 16, 2025\n:\nNew AI tool supports best practices to prevent spread of dangerous C. diff infections\n-- Emory News Center\n.\nJun 13, 2025\n:\nAI Tool Shows Promise for Antimicrobial Stewardship, C Diff Prevention in Hospitals\n-- HCPLive\n.\nJun 12, 2025\n:\nClinically deployed AI guidance for preventing C. difficile spread\n-- UMich CSE\n.\nSept 12, 2024\n:\nThese CSE PhD alums have accepted faculty positions\n-- UMich CSE\n.\nNov 10, 2022\n:\n2022 CSE Graduate Student Honors Competition highlights outstanding research\n-- UMich CSE\n.\nFeb 22, 2022\n:\nAI Triage\n-- Digital Health Wire newsletter\n.\nFeb 22, 2022\n:\nHow to train a clinical AI to predict bad health outcomes\n-- STAT Health Tech\n.\nFeb 17, 2022\n:\nOpen-source patient model tops industry standard\n-- University of Michigan News\n.\nDec 7, 2021\n:\nHow bias can creep into medical databanks that drive precision health and clinical AI\n-- Michigan Health Lab\n.\nJun 22, 2021\n:\nA hospital algorithm designed to predict a deadly condition misses most cases\n-- The Verge\n.\nApr 21, 2021\n:\nIn scramble to respond to Covid-19, hospitals turned to models with high risk of bias\n-- MedCityNews\n.\nMar 30, 2021\n:\nFrom collaboration to validation: How a team of engineers, clinicians, programmers, and administrators evaluated one of the most widely implemented predictive models\n-- Precision Health at UM\n.\nJan 22, 2021\n:\nEpic Deterioration Index May Identify High- and Low-Risk COVID-19 Patients\n-- Clinical Advisor\n.\nOct 12, 2020\n:\nNew framework helps streamline EHR data extraction\n-- Healthcare IT News\n.\nMay 12, 2020\n:\nFaster than COVID: a computer model that predicts the disease’s next move\n-- The Mighican Engineer News Center\n.\nApr 15, 2020\n:\nComputer scientists employ AI to help address COVID-19 challenges\n-- Michigan AI Lab\n.\nPublications (PhD-era)\nalso see\npublications of the lab\nOptimizing loop diuretic treatment for mortality reduction in patients with acute dyspnea using a practical offline reinforcement learning pipeline for healthcare: a retrospective single center simulation study\nJMIR Medical Informatics, Sep 2025\nJung Min Lee\n,\nShengpu Tang\n,\nMichael W. Sjoding\n,\nJenna Wiens\npaper\nGuiding\nClostridioides difficile\nInfection Prevention Efforts in a Hospital Setting With AI\nJAMA Network Open, Jun 2025\nShengpu Tang\n,\nStephanie Shepard\n,\nRebekah Clark\n,\nErkin Ötleş\n,\nChidimma Udegbunam\n,\nJosh Tran\n,\nMelinda Seiler\n,\nJustin Ortwine\n,\nAkbar K. Waljee\n,\nJerod Nagel\n,\nSarah L. Krein\n,\nJacob E. Kurlander\n,\nPaul J. Grant\n,\nJihoon Baang\n,\nAnastasia Wasylyshyn\n,\nKrishna Rao\n,\nJenna Wiens\npaper\nTargeting\nClostridioides difficile\nInfection Prevention Efforts with Artificial Intelligence\nID\nWeek Oral Abstract, Oct 2024; Published in\nOpen Forum Infectious Diseases\nShengpu Tang\n,\nRebekah Clark\n,\nStephanie Shepard\n,\nErkin Ötleş\n,\nMaxim Garifullin\n,\nMelinda Seiler\n,\nJustin Ortwine\n,\nPatrick Arnold\n,\nJerod Nagel\n,\nJeremy Jared\n,\nSarah L Krein\n,\nJacob E Kurlander\n,\nPaul J Grant\n,\nJihoon Baang\n,\nAnastasia Wasylyshyn\n,\nKrishna Rao\n,\nJenna Wiens\npaper\nCANDOR: Counterfactual ANnotated DOubly Robust off-policy evaluation\nunder review\nAishwarya Mandyam\n,\nShengpu Tang\n,\nJiayu Yao\n,\nEmma Brunskill\n,\nJenna Wiens\n,\nBarbara E Engelhardt\narXiv\nCounterfactual-Augmented Importance Sampling for Semi-Offline Policy Evaluation\nNeurIPS 2023\nShengpu Tang\n,\nJenna Wiens\npaper\ncode\nposter\nvideo\nwebsite\narXiv\nLeveraging Factored Action Spaces for Off-Policy Evaluation\nFrontiers4LCD @ ICML 2023, Counterfactuals @ ICML 2023\nAaman Peter Rebello\n,\nShengpu Tang\n,\nJenna Wiens\n,\nSonali Parbhoo\npaper\ncode\nTowards Data-Driven Offline Simulations for Online Reinforcement Learning\nOffline RL @ NeurIPS 2022\nShengpu Tang\n,\nFelipe Vieira Frujeri\n,\nDipendra Misra\n,\nAlex Lamb\n,\nJohn Langford\n,\nPaul Mineiro\n,\nSebastian Kochman\npreprint\ncode\nLeveraging Factored Action Spaces for Efficient Offline Reinforcement Learning in Healthcare\nNeurIPS 2022 (Oral); also at DARL @ ICML 2022\nShengpu Tang\n,\nMaggie Makar\n,\nMichael W. Sjoding\n,\nFinale Doshi-Velez\n,\nJenna Wiens\npa...[truncated]"
