  "serper::Lei Ma": {
    "homepage": "https://www.malei.org/",
    "emails": [
      "contact@momentum-lab.com",
      "ma.lei@acm.org"
    ],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Lei Ma\nAssociate Professor\nThe University of Tokyo, Japan\nUniversity of Alberta, Canada\nEmail: ma.lei [at] acm [dot] org\nProspective Students:\nMy research group is continuously recruiting. Currently, our lab have multiple scholarship & RA positions for Ph.D., MSc. candidates, and postdocs with competitive salary support, at both The University of Tokyo, Japan and\n                      University of Alberta, Canada. If you have a\nstrong background\nin the topics relevant (\nbut not limited\n) to my research with\nstrong self-motivation & strong will to achieve\n, I strongly encourage you to apply and join us.\nNote that to inquiry about opportunities to join our research group, please send emails\nonly to\ncontact@momentum-lab.com\nto inquiry and apply!\nFor students already with\nTodai\n, or\nstudents who can come to Todai Hongo Campus area\n, please simply drop me an email to schedule a meet up at the Campus, to discuss the possibilities.\nNEWS\n(03/2024) Our paper\nActive Code Learning: Benchmarking Sample-Efficient Training of Code Models\nis accepted at IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering (TSE).\n(02/2024) Our paper\nPromptCharm: Text-to-Image Generation through Multi-modal Prompting and Refinement\nis accepted at ACM CHI 2024. \n                        \n                        This paper proposes PromptCharm, a very early mixed-initiative and interactive system that facilitates text-to-image creation through multi-modal prompt engineering and refinement. It helps designers in prompting, automatically refines and optimizes the the initial prompt, and further supports in exploring and selecting different image styles within a large database with iterative refinments.\n(01/2024) Our paper\nISR-LLM: Iterative Self-Refined Large Language Model for Long-Horizon Sequential Task Planning\nis accepted at ICRA 2024. \n                        LLMs are advantageous in offering the potential to enhance the generalizability as task-agnostic planners and facilitate flexible interaction between human instructors and planning systems. However, task plans generated by LLMs often lack feasibility and correctness. To address this challenge, in this exploratory\n                        work, we designed a novel framework that improves LLM-based planning through an iterative self-refinement process towards appraoching better fesibility and trustworthiness.\n(01/2024) Our papers\nLRR: Language-Driven Resamplable Continuous Representation against Adversarial Tracking Attacks\nand\nNeuron Activation Coverage: Rethinking Out-of-distribution Detection and Generalization\nare accepted at ICLR 2024.\n(12/2023) Our paper\nMultiTest: Physical-Aware Object Insertion for Testing Multi-sensor Fusion Perception Systems\nis accepted at ICSE 2024. This is our exploratory \n                         work that proposes a testing framework for muti-sensor system by considering the multi-modality characteristics of such systems.\n(10/2023) Our paper\nLUNA: A Model-Based Universal Analysis Framework for Large Language Models\nis available on Arxiv. \n                         \nThis paper introduces the inaugural mode-based, general-purpose analysis framework designed to scrutinize, comprehend, and rationalize the behaviors and risks associated with inferences made by Large Language Models (LLMs) following a human-centered manner. LUNA is crafted with high extensibility in mind, and our ongoing efforts are geared towards adapting LUNA for various domain-specific tasks and ensuring its compliance with trustworthiness standards.\nWe would regularly updating more extensive findings. Should you have an interest in LUNA, for both researchers or industry practitioners, please do not hesitate to contact us. We welcome collaboration and are keen to explore the potential of LUNA together.\n(08/2023) Our paper\nLook Before You Leap: An Exploratory Study of Uncertainty Measurement for Large Language Models\nis available on Arxiv. \n                         \n                         This paper investigates blackbox techniques to understand the confidence of inference  by LLMs. We found that even under the blackbox setting, we can still gain intuition and understanding of the level of confidence in an LLM's inference at industrial scale. We will continuously update and release more comprehensive results.\n(08/2023) Our paper\nIs Model Attention Aligned with Human Attention? An Empirical Study on Large Language Models for Code Generation\nis available on Arxiv. This is an study\n                         for better understanding and constructing human-centered process of Large Language Models for Code Generation.\n(08/2023) Our paper\nTowards Building AI-CPS with NVIDIA Isaac Sim: An Industrial Benchmark and Case Study for Robotics Manipulation\nis available on Arxiv. This is our exploratory study towards sim to real, via efficiently building AI-CPS with Nvidia Isaac Sim as an early step.\n(08/2023) Three papers are accepted by\nESEC/FSE 2023\n.\n(08/2023) Three papers are accepted by\nASE 2023\n.\n(06/2023) Our paper\nSIEGE: A Semantics-Guided Safety Enhancement Framework for AI-Enabled Cyber-Physical Systems\nis accepted at\nTSE 2023\n. This paper performs an early explorative study for the safety enhancement of AI-CPS via ensembling approach, which is also a continuous series of work towards investigating safety assurance after our previous work on AI-CPS benchmarking (ICSE 2022), Falsifai (TSE 2022), etc.\n(06/2023) Our paper\nFaire: Repairing Fairness of Neural Networks via Neuron Condition Synthesis\nis accepted at\nACM TOSEM 2023\n.\n(04/2023) Our work\nArchRepair\non the Repair of Deep Neural Network and the recent research activities of JST eAI project\n                           has been reported by\n日本経済新聞 (Nikkei News)\n, also see\nhere\n.\n(04/2023) On April 2023, I joined computer science department of\nThe University of Tokyo, Japan\nas Associate Professor.\n(03/2023) Our paper\nEvading DeepFake Detectors via Adversarial Statistical Consistency\nis accepted at\nCVPR 2023\n.\n(02/2023) Our paper\nPatchCensor: Patch Robustness Certification for Transformers via Exhaustive Testing\nis accepted at\nACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology (TOSEM 2023)\n.\n(02/2023) Our paper\nArchRepair: Block-Level Architecture-Oriented Repairing for Deep Neural Networks\nis accepted at\nACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology (TOSEM 2023)\n.\n(01/2023) Our papers on human-centered interactive data debugging\nDeepLens\nand model debugging\nDeepSeer\nare accepted at\nCHI 2023\n.\n(01/2023) Our paper\nNeural Episodic Control with State Abstraction\nis accepted at\nICLR 2023 as a spotlight paper\n.\n(12/2022) Our paper\nGraphSearchNet: Enhancing GNNs via Capturing Global Dependencies for Semantic Code Search\nis accepted at\nIEEE Transactions on Software Engineering (TSE 2023)\n.\n(12/2022) Four papers accepted at\nICSE 2023\n(two on research track, one on SEIP track, one NIER track).\n(11/2022) Our paper\nDeepGemini: Verifying Dependency Fairness for Deep Neural Network\nis accepted at\nAAAI 2023\n.\n(07/2022) Join\nIEEE CIS Neural Networks Committee on Secure Learning\nas a Task Force Member.\n(07/2022) Our paper\nDecompiling x86 Deep Neural Network Executables\nis accepted at\nThe 32nd USENIX Security Symposium (USENIX Security '23 Summer)\n(07/2022) Our paper\nFalsifAI: Falsification of AI-Enabled Hybrid Control Systems Guided by Time-Aware Coverage Criteria\nis accepted at\nIEEE Transactions on Software Engineering (TSE)\n(06/2022) Our paper\nDARTSRepair: Core-Failure-Set Guided DARTS for Network Robustness to Common Corruptions\nis accepted at\nPattern Recognition 2022\n(06/2022) Our paper\nxFuzz: Machine Learning Guided Cross-Contract Fuzzing\nis accepted at\nIEEE Transactions on Dependable and Secure Computing, TDSC 2022\n(03/2022) Our paper\nCountering Malicious DeepFakes: Survey, Battleground, and Horizon\nis accepted at\nInternational Journal of Computer Vision, IJCV 2022\n. We present a comprehensive survey covering the latest DeepFake generation and detection methods, as well as the battleground landscape.\n(01/2022) Our paper\nWhen Cyber-Physical Systems Meet AI: A Benchmark, an Evaluation, and a Way Forward\nis accepted at\nICSE SEIP 2022\n, to the best of our knowledge, this is the very first systematic benchmark construction and study of\nAI-enabled Cyber Physical Systems (AI-CPS)\n. We make our dataset and benchmark publically available, and hope this would inspire lots of future new reserach and investigation along this exciting direction!\n(01/2022) Our paper\nAn Empirical Study on Data Distribution-Aware Test Selection for Deep Learning Enhancement\nis accepted at\nACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology (TOSEM 2022)\n.\n(12/2021) Our paper\nAn Empirical Study of the Impact of Hyperparameter Tuning and Model Optimization on DNN\nis accepted at\nACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology (TOSEM 2021)\n.\n(11/2021) Our paper\nTowards Robustness of Deep Program Processing Models -- Detection, Estimation and Enhancement\nis accepted at\nACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology (TOSEM 2021)\n.\n(09/2021) Our paper\nNPC: Neuron Path Coverage via Characterizing Decision Logic of Deep Neural Networks\nis accepted at\nACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology (TOSEM 2021)\n.\n(08/2021) Our paper\nDeepRepair: Style-Guided Repairing for Deep Neural Networks in the Real-World Operational Environment\nis accepted at\nIEEE Transactions on Reliability 2021\n.\n(07/2021) Our paper\nLearning to Adversarially Blur Visual Object Tracking\nis accepted at\nICCV 2021\n.\n(07/2021) Join\nIJCAI 2022\nas a PC Member.\n(07/2021) Our paper\nPredictive Perturbation-aware Filtering against Adversarial Attack via Multi-domain Learning\nis accepted at\nACM MM 2021\n.\n(07/2021) Our paper\nAn Empirical Study of GUI Widget Detection for Industrial Mobile Games\nis accepted at\nFSE 2021\nIndustry Track.\n(06/2021) Join\nICLR 2022\nas a reviewer,\nAISec 2021\nand\nESEC/FSE 2022\nas a PC member. Please consider submitting your high quality papers.\n(05/2021) Our paper\nAutomatic RNN R...[truncated]"
  },
