    "homepage_text": "Tian Luo | Old Dominion University\nSkip to main content\nApply\nVisit\nGive\nmyODU\nMenu\nSearch\nClose\nTian Luo\nProfessor\nDepartment of STEM Education & Professional Studies\nTian Luo\n757-683-5369\ntluo@odu.edu\nResearchGate\n2323 EDUCATION BUILDING\nNORFOLK,            23529\nBiography\nDr. Tian Luo is a Professor in the Instructional Design and Technology (IDT) program at the Darden College of Education, where she teaches graduate-level courses and conducts research in instructional design and technology. Prior to entering academia, she worked as an instructional design professional in both higher education and corporate settings.\nHer research interests focus on emerging technologies, open educational resources (OER), and generative AI in instructional design. She has published more than 70 refereed journal articles, along with numerous additional non-referred publications, including an edited book, practitioner articles, book chapters, and conference proceedings. Her scholarship has appeared in leading journals such as British Journal of Educational Technology, and Educational Technology Research and Development.\nDr. Luo currently serves as Co-Editor-in-Chief for the Journal of Information Technology Education: Research and Journal of Information Technology Education: Innovations in Practice. She is also the President-Elect of the Technology Integrated Learning (TIL) Division of the Association for Educational Communications and Technology (AECT).\nDr. Luo’s teaching, scholarship, and service have been recognized with awards from Old Dominion University, the American Educational Research Association (AERA), AECT, and the Informing Science Institute (ISI).\nEducation & Credentials\nPh.D.\nin Instructional Technology, Ohio University, (2014)\nM.A.\nin Communication and Development Studies, Ohio University, (2010)\nComputer-Assisted Language Learning (CALL) Certificate\nSponsoring Organization:\nDepartment of Linguistics, Ohio University\nDate Obtained:\n2013-05-01\nThe 21st Century Leadership Certificate\nSponsoring Organization:\nAmanda J. Cunningham Leadership Center, Ohio University\nDate Obtained:\n2010-02-01\nExpertise\nExpertise\nInstructional Design and Technology\nWeb 2.0\nSocial Media\nTwitter\nMicroblogging\nTechnology Integration\nComputer-Mediated Collaborative Learning\nResearch Interests\nGenAI, OER, UDL, social media, online teaching and learning, game-based learning, professional development, instructional design, technology integration\nSelect Publications\nArticles\nLi, B., Luo, T., Bahari, A., Yang, M. and Chan, W. Sha. (2025).  Beyond scores: a systematic review of learning and psychological outcomes of digital game-based language learning (2010–2025).\nInnovation in Language Learning and Teaching\n, pp. 1-37.\nMills, E., Stefaniak, J., Luo, T. and Glass, C. (2019).  An exploration of career decision-making among domestic and international instructional design students.\nTechTrends\n.\nLuo, T., Franklin, T., Moore, D. and Crompton, T. (2019).  Applying a modified technology acceptance model to qualitatively analyze the factors affecting microblogging integration.\nInternational Journal of Social Media and Interactive Learning Environments\n.\nBaaki, J. and Luo, T.  Chopped ID: Students Engaged in Gamification to Enhance Advanced Instructional Design Techniques. .\nJournal of Applied Instructional Design.\n.\nLuo, T., Moore, D. and Franklin, T. (2019).  Examining participation and engagement in a microblogging-supported college-level hybrid course.\nInnovations in Education and Teaching International\n, pp. 1-11.\nXie, Q. and Luo, T. (2019).  Examining User Participation and Network Structure via an Analysis of a Twitter-Supported Conference Backchannel.\nJournal of Educational Computing Research\n.\nMuljana , P. and Luo, T. (2019).  Factors contributing to student retention in online learning and recommended strategies for improvement: A systematic literature review.\nJournal of Information Technology Education: Research\n.\nLuo, T. and Xie, Q. (2019).  Using Twitter as a pedagogical tool in two classrooms: a comparative case study between an education and a communication class.\nJournal of Computing in Higher Education\n, pp. 1--24.\nLuo, T., Shah, S. and Cromptom, H. (2019).  Using Twitter to Support Reflective Learning in an Asynchronous Online Course.\nAustralasian Journal of Educational Technology\n.\nLuo, T. and Murray, A. (2018).  Connected Education: Teachers’ Attitudes towards Student Learning in a 1: 1 Technology Middle School Environment.\nJournal of Online Learning Research\n4 (1) , pp. 87--116.\nLuo, T. (2018).  Delving into the Specificity of Instructional Guidance in Social Media-supported Learning Environments.\nJournal of Information Technology Education: Innovations in Practice\n17 (1) , pp. 37--54.\nLuo, T., Murray, A. and Crompton, H. (2017).  Designing authentic learning activities to train pre-service teachers about teaching online.\nInternational Review of Research in Open and Distance Learning\n18 (7) , pp. 141-157.\nLuo, T. and Clifton, L. (2017).  Examining collaborative knowledge construction in microblogging-based learning environments.\nJournal of Information Technology Education: Research\n16 (1) , pp. 365-390.\nLuo, T., Lee, G. and Molina, C. (2017).   Incorporating Istation into early childhood classrooms to improve reading comprehension.\nJournal of Information Technology Education-Research\n16 , pp. 247-266.\nLuo, T., Hibbard, L., Franklin, T. and Moore, D. (2017).  Preparing Teacher Candidates for Virtual Field Placements via an Exposure to K-12 Online Teaching.\nJournal of Information Technology Education: Research\n16 , pp. 1–14.\nLuo, T., Sickel, J. and Cheng, L. (2017).  Preservice Teachers’ Participation and Perceptions of Twitter Live Chats as Personal Learning Networks.\nTechTrends: Linking Research & Practice to Improve Learning\n61 (3) , pp. 226-235.\nBaaki, J. and Luo, T. (2017).  Stimulating Students’ Use of External Representations for a Distance Education Time Machine Design.\nTechTrends: Linking Research & Practice to Improve Learning\n61 (4) , pp.  355-365.\nLuo, T., Dani, D. and Cheng, L. (2017).  Viability of using Twitter to support peer instruction in teacher education..\nInternational Journal of Social Media and Interactive Learning Environments (IJSMILE).\n.\nLuo, T. (2016).  Enabling microblogging-based peer feedback in face-to-face classrooms.\nInnovations in Education and Teaching International\n53 (2) , pp. 156-166.\nHarring, K. and Luo, T. (2016).  Eportfolios: Supporting Reflection and Deep Learning in High-Impact Practices.\nPeer Review\n18 (3) , pp. 9-12.\nLuo, T. (2015).  Instructional guidance in microblogging-supported learning: insights from a multiple case study.\nJournal of Computing in Higher Education\n27 (3) , pp. 173-194.\nLuo, T. and Franklin, T. (2015).  Tweeting and Blogging: Moving towards Education 2.0.\nInternational Journal on E-Learning\n14 (2) , pp. 235-258.\nCopp, S. E., Fischer, R. L., Luo, T., Moore, D. R. and Dikkers, S. (2014).  Analyzing Commercial Video Game Instruction through the Lens of  Instructional Design.\nThe Journal of Applied Instructional Design\n4 (1) , pp. 79-90.\nWilliams, II, Robert, L., Karadogan, E., Moore, D. R. and Luo, T. (2013).  Design, Development And Evaluation of An Interactive Virtual Haptics-augmented Training System for Undergraduate Engineering.\nMoore, D., Robert, W. L., Luo, T. and Ernur , K. (2013).  Elusive Achievement Effects of Haptic Feedback.\nJournal of Interactive Learning Research\n24 (3) , pp. 329-347.\nLuo, T., Gao, F. and Hoff, K. S. (2013).  Examining Student Participation in Three Learning Activities Supported by Social Annotation Tools.\nJournal of Educational Technology Development and Exchange\n6 (2) , pp. 53-68.\nLuo, T. (2013).  Web 2.0 for Language Learning: Benefits and Challenges for Educators.\nInternational Journal of Computer-Assisted Language Learning and Teaching (IJCALLT)\n3 (3) , pp. 1-17.\nLuo, T. and Gao, F. (2012).  Enhancing classroom learning experience by providing structures to microblogging-based activities.\nJournal of Information Technology Education: Innovations in Practice\n11 (1) , pp. 199-211.\nGao, F., Luo, T. and Zhang, K. (2012).   Tweeting for learning: A critical analysis of research on microblogging in education published in 2008-2011.\nBritish Journal of Educational Technology\n43 (5) , pp. 783-801.\nBook Chapters\nLuo, T. and Baaki, J. (2019).  Scaffolding Problem-Solving and Instructional Design Processes: Engaging Students in Reflection-in-Action and External Representations in Three Online Courses\nStudent-Centered Virtual Learning Environments in Higher Education\n(pp. 40--69) IGI Global.\nLuo, T. and Gao, F. (2013).  Designing Microblogging-Based Class Activities with Twiducate AVM - Akademische Verlagsgemeinschaft Munchen.\nConference Proceeding\nDousay, T. A., Asino, T., Luo, T., Krutka, D. H., Greenhalgh, S., Rodesiler, L. and Walster, D. (2018).  Social media & teacher professional development Washington, DC: Society for Information Technology & Teacher Education International Conference 2018.\nCopp, S., Fisher, R., Luo, T., Dikkers, S. and Moore, D. (2013).  Batman Meets Gagne: Analyzing Commercial Video Game Instruction through the Lens of Instructional Design (pp. 1102-1108) World Conference on E-Learning in Corporate, Government, Healthcare, and Higher Education.\nLuo, T., Gao, F. and Sun, Y. (2013).  Using social annotation tools to foster collaborative learning (pp. 3284-3287) Society for information technology & teacher education international conference.\nBuchman, S., Snider, P., Luo, T. and Gondwe, K. (2013).  Using Twitter to facilitate case-based instruction in a nursing classroom (pp. 3066-3071) Society for Information Technology & Teacher Education International Conference.\nSun, Y., Franklin, T. and Luo, T. (2013).  Using Wikis to Support Peer Assessment Activities in Higher Education (pp. 3388-3392) Society for Information Technology & Teacher Education International Conference.\nLuo, T. and Franklin, T. (2012).  \"You got to be foll...[truncated]"
  },
