    "homepage_text": "Junchen YE's Homepage\nSearch\n👋 Welcome to My Homepage\nI have experienced five years of good time at the foot of Yuelu Mountain.\nThere are my\nfavorite person\nand\nunforgettable places\nin this photo, which shot on a warm afternoon.\n(Last update: Mar 18, 2022)\nJunchen(Eric) YE\nPh.D. student\nThe Chinese University of Hong Kong (CUHK)\nBiography\nI am a yr1 PhD student at the Department of Physics, The Chinese University of Hong Kong (CUHK). My research interests include developing some robust hardwares for quantum sensing. and I’m also pursuing a chance to develop some awesome quantum technologies for future.\nInterests\nTopological Physics\nQuantum Technology\n(QC & QS)\nCondensed Matter Theory\nEducation\nPh.D student. in Physics, 2021-Now\nThe Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR, P.R. China\nB.Sc. in Applied Physics, 2015-2020\nHunan University, P. R. China\nHonor & Awards\nAs time flows, they will be added or deleted dynamically.\n(Listed in time order)\nHong Kong PhD Fellowship (HKPFS)\nResearch Grants Council, Hong Kong SAR, P. R. China\nAug 2021\n– Aug 2025\n2018-2019 China National Scholarship\nMinistry of Education, P. R. China\nDec 2019\n2019 MCM/ICM Contest Honorable Mention (Top 22.75%)\nConsortium for Mathematics and Its Applications (COMAP)\nMar 2019\n2017-2018 China National Scholarship\nMinistry of Education, P. R. China\nNov 2018\n2018 National Student Innovation and Entrepreneurship Training Program (\nProgram No.201810532235\n)\nDepartment of Higher Education, MOE of P. R. China\nOct 2018\n– Apr 2020\nResearch Experience\nUndergraduate\nExperimental Quantum Sensing\nProf. Ren-Bao LIU (Supervisior) Prof. Quan LI (Co-Supervisior)\nAug 2021 –\nAug 2023\nHong Kong SAR, P. R. China\nI’m building an experimental setup for widefield nanodiamond quantum sensing, which will provide a new approach to explore live cells. (In Progressing…)\nDynamical Classiﬁcation of Topological quantum Phases\nProf. Fuxiang LI (Supervisior)\nDec 2019 –\nJul 2020\nChangsha, P. R. China\nDynamical classiﬁcation of topological quantum phases by quench dynamics provides a novel and eﬃcient scheme in characterizing topological invariants in equilibrium. Diﬀerent from usual sudden quench, we study the non-adiabatic dynamical classiﬁcation scheme by a generic non-adiabatic protocol of slowly quenching the system Hamiltonian, which is realized by introducing a Coulomb-like Landau Zener problem. the new scheme can provides a more eﬃcient dynamical characterization approach for ultracold-atom experiments.\nUsing DFT to study topological surface states\nProf. Yexin FENG (Supervisior)\nOct 2019 –\nDec 2019\nChangsha, P. R. China\nTaking the 3D strong topological insulator Bi_{2}Se_{3} as an example, I learn the whole process from VASP (based on DFT) to constructing Wannier function, and ﬁnally using WannierTools to study topological properties, e.g., energy spectrum or spin texture of the surface states. computational results are consistent with experiments by ARPES.\nQuantum Mechanical Treatment of Single-slit Interference\nProf. Quan-Hui LIU (Supervisior)\nDec 2018 –\nFeb 2019\nChangsha, P. R. China\nMarcella published a “quantum-mechanical\" treatment of the single-slit interference experiment by assuming wave function is a plateau within a slit. we point out that he made a mistake (inﬁnitely large energy). by correcting wave function with the ground state of the inﬁnitely deep well, we get a new results which are in qualitatively agreement with the real experiment of material particles C_{60} .\nStudy on Suppression of Parametric Instabilities by Complex Velocity Modulation\nDr. Chenzhuo Xiao (Supervisior)\nOct 2017 –\nJun 2019\nChangsha, P. R. China\nWe numerically simulate the Stimulated Raman Scattering growing process under Flatten Maxwellian distribution and Double Maxwellian distribution by Vlasov program. In the linear phase, We found a linear increasing law between the average reﬂect radio and the temperature of hot electrons, furthermore, for which we provide an explanation with the Landau damping.\nRecent Posts\nNot Yet\nTest page\nIt is just a test, no content.\nJunchen(Eric) YE\n,\nYi Ding\nLast updated on\nDec 4, 2020\n1 min read\nDemo\n,\nTest\nSee all blog posts\nProjects\nNot yet. Maybe I can record here some challenges I will face during the process of building experimental platform in future.\nRecent & Upcoming Talks\nGroup Seminar in Westlake University\nEmergent topology under slow nonadiabatic quantum dynamics\nAug 18, 2020 1:00 PM — 3:00 PM\nWestlake University\nSlides\n2020 Hunan Provincial Physics Society Summer Academic Report\nEmergent topology under slow nonadiabatic quantum dynamics\nJul 26, 2020 1:00 PM — 3:00 PM\nOnline\nSlides\nVideo\nFeatured Publications\nI need more time.\nJunchen(Eric) YE\n,\nFuxiang Li\nJuly 2020\nPRA, ‘Editors’ Suggestion'\nEmergent topology under slow nonadiabatic quantum dynamics\nMy graduation project, “Emergent topology under slow nonadiabatic quantum dynamics” published on\nPhysical Review A\nas “\nEditors’ Suggestion\n” in Oct 2020.\nPDF\nCite\nSource Document\nDOI\nRecent Publications\nQuickly discover relevant content by\nfiltering publications\n.\nEmergent topology under slow nonadiabatic quantum dynamics\nMy graduation project, “Emergent topology under slow nonadiabatic quantum dynamics” published on\nPhysical Review A\nas “\nEditors’ Suggestion\n” in Oct 2020.\nJunchen(Eric) YE\n,\nFuxiang Li\nPDF\nCite\nSource Document\nDOI\nPopular Topics\nAcademic\nLZ Transition\nQuantum Quench\nTest\nTogological Phases\nUltra-cold atom\nContact\nName\nEmail\nMessage\nDo not fill this field unless you are a bot:\nSend\nMa Liu Shui, Shatin, N.T., Hong Kong SAR 999077\nLG2, Run-Run Shaw Science Building, CUHK\nBook an appointment\nWeChat ID: JC_YE_Eric\nCite\n×\nCopy\nDownload"
  },
