  "serper::Colleen Alkalay-Houlihan": {
    "homepage": "https://cra.org/about/awards/outstanding-undergraduate-researcher-award/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Outstanding Undergraduate Researcher Award - CRA\nUniting industry, academia and government to advance computing research and change the world.\nLink to LinkedIn\nLink to Facebook\nLink to Youtube\nLink to Rss  this site\nCRA Outstanding Undergraduate Researcher Award\nThis award program recognizes undergraduate students in North American colleges and universities who show outstanding research potential in an area of computing research. Click\nhere\nfor nomination information.\nLearn more about the nomination process\nRecipients\n2026\n2026\nSelection Committee:\nJonathan Bell (Northeastern University) and Evan Peck (University of Colorado Boulder), who led this year’s efforts. CRA is also grateful to Alex Psomas (Purdue University), Bo Zhu (Georgia Institute of Technology), Brittany Duncan (University of Nebraska-Lincoln), George Montanez (Harvey Mudd College), Josh San Miguel (University of Wisconsin-Madison), Marynel Vazquez (Yale University), Michael Spear (Lehigh University), Rahmat Beheshti (University of Delaware), Rose Yu (University of California, San Diego) for volunteering their time to review and acknowledge this year’s nominees.\nAwardees\nAhan Mishra\nCornell University\nAhan Mishra, a junior Computer Science student at Cornell, excels in theoretical computer science by developing advanced algorithms for problems like recurring task scheduling (pinwheel scheduling). He uniquely solo-authored a paper accepted to SODA, a premier theory conference, by solving a difficult, long-standing conjecture on the pinwheel covering problem using novel simplification techniques. He also designed a highly effective polynomial-time approximation scheme for a related problem. Beyond theory, he researches machine learning topics like generative models and methods to evade LLM dataset inference. He demonstrates strong leadership as the Co-President of the Cornell AI club and Head of Quantum Algorithms, in addition to his role as a Teaching Assistant.\nChau Vu\nPomona College\nChau Vu is a senior at Pomona College, pursuing a B.A. in Computer Science and Math, with a strong focus on Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) and multisensory systems. She is a recognized leader, having received the UIST Best Paper Award for co-authoring a tool that scaffolds drawing, and an ACM CHI Best Paper Honorable Mention for her first-authored theoretical framework on temporal reflection in personal informatics through baby tracking. Chau is currently conceiving and implementing her undergraduate thesis, PianoSense, a multisensory system using a haptic glove and 3D hand modeling to improve remote piano instruction. She was a Microsoft Software Engineering Intern and is a Computing Research Association UR2PhD Scholar. Additionally, she co-founded VieSign, a Vietnamese Sign Language learning platform, demonstrating a strong commitment to using technology for social impact.\nHaoquan Fang\nUniversity of Washington\nHaoquan Fang is a senior undergraduate at the University of Washington, majoring in Computer Science and Statistics. His research focuses on building generalist robotic manipulation policies with spatial intelligence using foundation models. He co-led MolmoAct, an Action Reasoning Model (ARM) that integrates perception, planning, and control, outperforming competitive models from large corporations, and was recognized as setting the standard for embodied reasoning. He also led the SAM2Act project, developing a model that leverages visual foundation models and a novel memory architecture for robotic manipulation. His work has resulted in publications at top conferences like ICML and was selected for a podium presentation at a workshop. Beyond research, he is dedicated to service, having co-organized a CVPR workshop and served as a Teaching Assistant and mentor.\nLuisa Mao\nThe University of Texas at Austin\nLuisa Mao is a senior in the honors program at the University of Texas at Austin, double majoring in Computer Science and Mathematics with a minor in Robotics. Her research focuses on advancing robot perception and learning for autonomous systems in complex environments. Her key contributions include: leading the PACER project on preference-conditioned all-terrain costmap generation, which was published in a top journal; developing a novel visual localization algorithm that significantly improved NASA’s Astrobee robot operations; and co-authoring a paper on social navigation for mobile robots during her freshman year. She has been offered a full-time position by the Tesla Optimus AI team following an internship and is a recipient of both the Goldwater and Astronaut Scholarships.\nQingyun Yang\nVanderbilt University\nQingyun Yang, a Computer Science and Mathematics major at Vanderbilt University, specializes in applying Machine Learning and computer vision to clinical problems, especially in surgical guidance. She led a project using Augmented Reality and deformable registration for head and neck cancer surgery, which significantly improved tumor location accuracy over rigid registration. This research, which involved creating a novel registration pipeline, resulted in a first-author paper at MICCAI, earning a podium presentation. She also works on generative modeling for tumor progression and CT image analysis. Outside the lab, she serves as a Teaching Assistant, mentors students, and is a Stanford SERGE scholar.\nRanya Sharma\nUniversity of Chicago\nRanya Sharma is an undergraduate at the University of Chicago, double majoring in Computer Science and Political Science. Her research focuses on Internet infrastructure, security, and privacy, bridging technical work with policy implications. She has led research on encrypted Domain Name System (DNS) privacy, user interfaces, and the performance limitations of home WiFi networks. Her study on WiFi showed that wireless networks often bottleneck speeds for users with very fast internet connections. She is involved in leadership (Tech Policy branch of Tech Team and student liaison) at the University of Chicago Institute of Politics.\nSophia Tang\nUniversity of Pennsylvania\nSophia Tang is a junior undergraduate at the University of Pennsylvania, dual-majoring in Engineering and Economics. Her research is concentrated on creating new generative algorithms for biomolecular sequence design. Within just one year, she has produced six original, first-author paper submissions, including the multi-objective discrete diffusion model PepTune and a framework called Branched Schrödinger Bridge Matching for modeling how cells divide and change. Her work is considered pioneering in discrete generative methods. She is regarded by her advisor as highly productive and mature, already leading projects and mentoring other students.\nVictoria Li\nHarvard University\nVictoria Li is an undergraduate at Harvard, pursuing a BAin Computer Science, Statistics, and Cognitive Science. Her research focuses on understanding, predicting, and debugging neural networks. She led projects demonstrating that chatbot safety systems exhibit systematic biases based on user demographics and context. Notably, her work finding cases where providing data visualizations to AI systems can significantly improve a model’s data analysis, opens a new area of study. Additionally, she developed a method using a model’s internal structure to predict its generalization behavior in new situations. Beyond her research, she mentors and teaches students across math, computer science, and statistics.\nRunner Ups:\nChristopher Kverne, Florida International University\nHugo Garrido-Lestache Belinchon, Milwaukee School of Engineering\nIan Zhang, Duke University\nJiani Liu, University of Rochester\nJosh Yang, Brown University\nRyan Punamiya, Georgia Institute of Technology\nTavonput Luangphasy, Washington State University Vancouver\nJohn Dunbar, The University of Texas at Austin\nFinalists:\nDuc Nguyen Tien Vu, Michigan State University\nHao Xu, University of Washington\nJoonhyuk Ko, University of Virginia\nNidhi Begur, Florida Atlantic University\nQuentin Romero Lauro, University of Pittsburgh\nShubham Mohole, Cornell University\nSzymon Snoeck, Columbia University\nVictoria Phelps, University of California, Berkeley\nZachary Eisbach, Northeastern University\nHonorable Mentions:\nAaron Lin, University of Kentucky\nAbhinav Komanduri, University of Arkansas\nAdithya Bhaskara, University of Colorado Boulder\nAdrian Faust, University of New Mexico\nAdvay Singh, University of Michigan\nAdya Daruka, University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign\nAlbert Vo, Arizona State University\nAlec Fishbach, Arizona State University\nAlexander Portland, Brown University\nAlexandra Barancikova, Tufts University\nAlice Moayyedi, University of Waterloo\nAlvin Zhang, Johns Hopkins University\nAmber Fei, Carnegie Mellon University\nAmish Sethi, University of Pennsylvania\nAn Ngo, Bucknell University\nAnas Saeed, University of California, Merced\nAndrew Gusty, University of Colorado Boulder\nAndrew Kang, Cornell University\nAndrew Morgan, University of North Carolina at Charlotte\nAnirud Aggarwal, University of Maryland\nAnisha Tehim, Cornell University\nAnran Li, University of Michigan – Ann Arbor\nAnthony Arnold, University of Kentucky\nAntonio Lazaro, Temple University\nAnya Chernova, UC San Diego\nArda Uzunoğlu , Johns Hopkins University\nArinjay Singh, Northeastern University\nAshfin Rahman, Temple University\nAyushmaan Puri, University of California, San Diego\nBarış Bayazit, University of Toronto\nBeihao Zhou, University of Waterloo\nBenjamin Peter, University of Missouri\nBerelian Karimian, University of Michigan\nBilal Afzal Shafi, Cornell University\nBorngreat Omoma-Edosa, University of California, San Diego\nBoyi Zhang, University of Rochester\nBrandon Collins, Auburn University\nBrian Liu, University of California, San Diego\nBryce Bernstein, Carleton College\nCaelum van Ispelen, University of Utah\nCameron Scarpati, Vanderbilt University\nCat Lewin, University of Missouri-Kansas City\nChengyu (Cathy) Fan, Colby College\nChris Shang, University of California, San ...[truncated]"
  },
  "serper::Ewout Van Den Berg": {
