    "homepage_text": "Ewout van den Berg - IBM Research\nEwout van den Berg\nBio\nEwout van den Berg received his MSc degree at the Faculty of Electrical Engineering, Mathematics and Computer Science at Delft University of Technology, and his PhD degree at the Department of Computer Science at the University of British Columbia. He was a postdoctoral fellow at the Mathematics Department and Statistics Department at Stanford university from 2010 to 2013, and is now a research staff member of the Quantum Computing group at IBM T.J. Watson Research Center.\nPublications\nClassically propagating noise for faster error mitigation\nAndrew Eddins\nCaleb Johnson\net al.\n2026\nAPS Global Physics Summit 2026\nTalk\nCAESER: Circuit Average Ensemble Series Expansion Rescaling\nSwarnadeep Majumder\nBrad Mitchell\net al.\n2026\nAPS Global Physics Summit 2026\nTalk\nNoise characterization and error mitigation on IBM Heron processors: Part 1\nYoungseok Kim\nBrad Mitchell\net al.\n2025\nAPS Global Physics Summit 2025\nTalk\nNoise characterization and error mitigation on IBM Heron processors: Part 2\nBrad Mitchell\nYoungseok Kim\net al.\n2025\nAPS Global Physics Summit 2025\nTalk\nProbabilistic error cancellation for dynamic quantum circuits\nRiddhi Swaroop Gupta\nEwout Van Den Berg\net al.\n2024\nAPS March Meeting 2024\nTalk\nRecent progress and challenges in error mitigation with fixed-frequency superconducting quantum processors\nYoungseok Kim\nAndrew Eddins\net al.\n2023\nAPS March Meeting 2023\nInvited talk\nProbabilistic error cancellation with sparse Pauli-Lindblad models on noisy quantum processors\nEwout Van Den Berg\nZlatko Minev\net al.\n2023\nAPS March Meeting 2023\nInvited talk\nProbabilistic error cancellation for measurement-based circuits\nRiddhi Swaroop Gupta\nEwout Van Den Berg\net al.\n2023\nAPS March Meeting 2023\nTalk\nExperimentally characterizing error mitigation on large quantum circuits\nAndrew Eddins\nYoungseok Kim\net al.\n2023\nAPS March Meeting 2023\nTalk\nVisit Google Scholar\nPatents\n15 Dec 2025\nUS\n12499379\nReadout-error Mitigation For Quantum Expectation\n24 Nov 2025\nJP\n7779635\nReadout-error Mitigation For Quantum Expectation\n03 Nov 2025\nCN\nZL202180080689.1\nReadout-error Mitigation For Quantum Expectation\n06 Oct 2025\nUS\n12437226\nProbabalistic Amplification And Attenuation Of Quantum Errors Using A Single Dataset\n28 Jul 2025\nUS\n12373725\nSingle-shot Error Mitigation For Clifford Circuits\n14 Oct 2024\nUS\n12118435\nPauli-lindbladian Based Noise Modeling And Mitigation\n04 Sep 2024\nAU\n2021399066\nReadout-error Mitigation For Quantum Expectation\n09 Jan 2023\nUS\n11551131\nHamiltonian Simulation Based On Simultaneous-diagonalization\n15 Nov 2021\nUS\n11176478\nMethod For Estimating A Quantum Phase\n12 Aug 2019\nUS\n10380484\nAnnealed Dropout Training Of  Neural Networks\nBlog posts\nWith fault tolerance the ultimate goal, error mitigation is the path that gets quantum computing to usefulness\nDeep Dive\nKristan Temme, Ewout van den Berg, Abhinav Kandala, and Jay Gambetta\n19 Jul 2022\nQuantum Error Correction & Mitigation\nQuantum Research\nQuantum Software\nQuantum Systems\nTop collaborators\nFT\nFrancesco Tacchino\nResearch Staff Member\nMT\nMaika Takita\nPrincipal Research Scientist - QEC and Dynamic Circuits Experiments\nJG\nJim Garrison\nDeveloper, Quantum-Centric Supercomputing\nKS\nKunal Sharma\nSenior Research Scientist; Manager, Quantum Algorithms\nWork location\nIBM Research - Yorktown Heights\nYorktown Heights, NY USA\nMore publications\nGoogle Scholar\nOverview\nPublications\nBlog posts\nCollaborators"
  },
  "serper::Ryan-Rhys Griffiths": {
    "homepage": "https://ryan-rhys.com/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": "https://github.com/huawei-noah",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryan-rhys-griffiths-689b73128",
    "homepage_text": "Ryan-Rhys Griffiths\nRyan-Rhys Griffiths\nWelcome to my\npersonal website\nand thank you for taking the time to visit!\nOverview\nI’m a Research Scientist working on AI in San Francisco. My current focus is on large reasoning models and in particular their capabilities in domains such as\nmathematics\n,\nchemistry\n, and chess. Recently, after working in AI and machine learning for 12 years, I am becoming increasingly concerned about AI safety and the future of work.\nOther topics that interest me include probabilistic machine learning and AI4Science. I have worked on these topics at\nMeta\n,\nHuawei Noah’s Ark Lab\n,\nMILA\n, and the\nUniversity of Cambridge\n, where I did my\nPhD\nin the Theory of Condensed Matter Group at the\nCavendish Laboratory\n. Prior to that I did my undergrad in chemical physics at\nImperial College London\n.\nFor a more granular picture of my research interests see my\nGoogle Scholar page\n.\nMiscellaneous Facts\nI have an\nErdos number\nof 4 (\nAlexei Kornyshev\n->\nAbraham Nitzan\n->\nPeter Salamon\n->\nPaul Erdos\nbeing one route) and a\nBacon number\nof 3 (Jeremy Paxman -> Colin Firth -> Kevin Bacon), making my\nErdos-Bacon number\n7.\nIn a previous life I played\nchess\n.\nIn a previous life I liked to\nquiz\n.\nIn a current life I enjoy\nrunning\n.\nIn a current life I like to dance and I am the owner of a Bachata\ndance\ncompany.\nResearch Collaboration\nI receive a huge volume of emails and LinkedIn messages each day so please accept my apologies if I can’t reply to yours! Nonetheless, I have bandwidth for a small number of research collaborations. If you think I may be a useful supervisor for your project please see the instructions in\ncontact\n!\nNews\nOct 17, 2025\nI received a top reviewer award (top 8%) for\nNeurIPS 2025\n.\nAug 22, 2025\nI will serve as a reviewer for\nAISTATS 2026\n.\nAug 01, 2025\nI have launched my new dance company\nBachata Lab\n. Join us for weekly Friday classes at Smuin Ballets, 1830 17th Street, San Francisco, CA 94103 8pm-9:30pm. Beginners welcome! No prior experience needed!\nJul 20, 2025\nI will serve as an Area Chair for the\n2025 NeurIPS Workshop on AI4Science\n.\nApr 22, 2025\nI received an outstanding reviewer award (top 5%) for\nAISTATS 2025\n.\nSelected Publications\nMathematical Capabilities of ChatGPT\nSimon Frieder, Luca Pinchetti,\nRyan-Rhys Griffiths\n, and\n4 more authors\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n, 2024\nAbs\nBib\nHTML\nPDF\nVideo\nWe investigate the mathematical capabilities of two versions of ChatGPT (released 9-January-2023 and 30-January-2023) and of GPT-4 by testing them on publicly available datasets, as well as hand-crafted ones, using a novel evaluation scheme. In contrast to formal mathematics, where large databases of formal proofs are available (e.g., mathlib, the Lean Mathematical Library), current datasets of natural-language mathematics used to benchmark language models either cover only elementary mathematics or are very small. We address this by publicly releasing two new datasets: GHOSTS and miniGHOSTS. These are the first natural-language datasets curated by working researchers in mathematics that (1) aim to cover graduate-level mathematics, (2) provide a holistic overview of the mathematical capabilities of language models, and (3) distinguish multiple dimensions of mathematical reasoning. These datasets test, by using 1636 human expert evaluations, whether ChatGPT and GPT-4 can be helpful assistants to professional mathematicians by emulating use cases that arise in the daily professional activities of mathematicians. We benchmark the models on a range of fine-grained performance metrics. For advanced mathematics, this is the most detailed evaluation effort to date. We find that ChatGPT and GPT-4 can be used most successfully as mathematical assistants for querying facts, acting as mathematical search engines and knowledge base interfaces. GPT-4 can additionally be used for undergraduate-level mathematics but fails on graduate-level difficulty. Contrary to many positive reports in the media about GPT-4 and ChatGPT’s exam-solving abilities (a potential case of selection bias), their overall mathematical performance is well below the level of a graduate student. Hence, if you aim to use ChatGPT to pass a graduate-level math exam, you would be better off copying from your average peer!\n@article\n{\n2024_Simon\n,\ntitle\n=\n{Mathematical Capabilities of Chat{GPT}}\n,\nauthor\n=\n{Frieder, Simon and Pinchetti, Luca and Griffiths, Ryan-Rhys and Salvatori, Tommaso and Lukasiewicz, Thomas and Petersen, Philipp and Berner, Julius}\n,\njournal\n=\n{Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems}\n,\nvolume\n=\n{36}\n,\nyear\n=\n{2024}\n,\nbooktitle\n=\n{Proceedings of the 37th International Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems}\n,\nurl\n=\n{https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/file/58168e8a92994655d6da3939e7cc0918-Paper-Datasets_and_Benchmarks.pdf}\n,\ndimensions\n=\n{true}\n,\ndoi\n=\n{10.48550/arXiv.2301.13867}\n,\n}\nTraining a Scientific Reasoning Model for Chemistry\nSiddharth M Narayanan, James D Braza,\nRyan-Rhys Griffiths\n, and\n6 more authors\narXiv preprint arXiv:2506.17238\n, 2025\nBib\nHTML\nPDF\n@article\n{\n2025_Narayanan\n,\ntitle\n=\n{Training a Scientific Reasoning Model for Chemistry}\n,\nauthor\n=\n{Narayanan, Siddharth M and Braza, James D and Griffiths, Ryan-Rhys and Bou, Albert and Wellawatte, Geemi and Ramos, Mayk Caldas and Mitchener, Ludovico and Rodriques, Samuel G and White, Andrew D}\n,\njournal\n=\n{arXiv preprint arXiv:2506.17238}\n,\nyear\n=\n{2025}\n,\nurl\n=\n{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.17238}\n,\ndimensions\n=\n{true}\n,\n}\nAviary: training language agents on challenging scientific tasks\nSiddharth Narayanan, James D Braza,\nRyan-Rhys Griffiths\n, and\n8 more authors\narXiv preprint arXiv:2412.21154\n, 2024\nAbs\nBib\nHTML\nPDF\nSolving complex real-world tasks requires cycles of actions and observations. This is particularly true in science, where tasks require many cycles of analysis, tool use, and experimentation. Language agents are promising for automating intellectual tasks in science because they can interact with tools via natural language or code. Yet their flexibility creates conceptual and practical challenges for software implementations, since agents may comprise non-standard components such as internal reasoning, planning, tool usage, as well as the inherent stochasticity of temperature-sampled language models. Here, we introduce Aviary, an extensible gymnasium for language agents. We formalize agents as policies solving language-grounded partially observable Markov decision processes, which we term language decision processes. We then implement five environments, including three challenging scientific environments: (1) manipulating DNA constructs for molecular cloning, (2) answering research questions by accessing scientific literature, and (3) engineering protein stability. These environments were selected for their focus on multi-step reasoning and their relevance to contemporary biology research. Finally, with online training and scaling inference-time compute, we show that language agents backed by open-source, non-frontier LLMs can match and exceed both frontier LLM agents and human experts on multiple tasks at up to 100x lower inference cost.\n@article\n{\n2024_Narayanan\n,\ntitle\n=\n{Aviary: training language agents on challenging scientific tasks}\n,\nauthor\n=\n{Narayanan, Siddharth and Braza, James D and Griffiths, Ryan-Rhys and Ponnapati, Manu and Bou, Albert and Laurent, Jon and Kabeli, Ori and Wellawatte, Geemi and Cox, Sam and Rodriques, Samuel G and others}\n,\njournal\n=\n{arXiv preprint arXiv:2412.21154}\n,\nyear\n=\n{2024}\n,\ndimensions\n=\n{true}\n,\nurl\n=\n{https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.21154}\n,\n}\nGAUCHE: a Library for Gaussian Processes in Chemistry\nRyan-Rhys Griffiths\n, Leo Klarner, Henry Moss, and\n8 more authors\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems\n, 2024\nAbs\nBib\nHTML\nPDF\nVideo\nWe introduce GAUCHE, an open-source library for GAUssian processes in CHEmistry. Gaussian processes have long been a cornerstone of probabilistic machine learning, affording particular advantages for uncertainty quantification and Bayesian optimisation. Extending Gaussian processes to molecular representations, however, necessitates kernels defined over structured inputs such as graphs, strings and bit vectors. By providing such kernels in a modular, robust and easy-to-use framework, we seek to enable expert chemists and materials scientists to make use of state-of-the-art black-box optimization techniques. Motivated by scenarios frequently encountered in practice, we showcase applications for GAUCHE in molecular discovery, chemical reaction optimisation and protein design. The codebase is made available at https://github.com/leojklarner/gauche.\n@article\n{\n2024_gauche\n,\ntitle\n=\n{{GAUCHE}: a Library for {Gaussian} Processes in Chemistry}\n,\nauthor\n=\n{Griffiths, Ryan-Rhys and Klarner, Leo and Moss, Henry and Ravuri, Aditya and Truong, Sang and Du, Yuanqi and Stanton, Samuel and Tom, Gary and Rankovic, Bojana and Jamasb, Arian and others}\n,\njournal\n=\n{Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems}\n,\nvolume\n=\n{36}\n,\nyear\n=\n{2024}\n,\ndimensions\n=\n{true}\n,\nurl\n=\n{https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/file/f2b1b2e974fa5ea622dd87f22815f423-Paper-Conference.pdf}\n,\n}\nGaussian Processes at Extreme Lengthscales: From Molecules to Black Holes\nRyan-Rhys Griffiths\nUniversity of Cambridge\n, 2023\nAbs\nBib\nHTML\nPDF\nIn many areas of the observational and experimental sciences data is scarce. Observation in high-energy astrophysics is disrupted by celestial occlusions and limited telescope time while laboratory experiments in synthetic chemistry and materials science are both time and cost-intensive. On the other hand, knowledge about the data-generation mechanism is often available in the experimental sciences, such as the measurement error of a piece of laboratory apparatus. Both characteristics make Gaussian processes (GPs) ideal candidates for fitting such datasets. GPs can make predictions with considerat...[truncated]"
  },
  "serper::Nino Antulov-Fantulin": {
    "homepage": "https://www.ninoaf.com/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": "https://ch.linkedin.com/in/nino-antulov-fantulin-91a5b0a",
    "homepage_text": "Home | Mysite\ntop of page\nNino Antulov-Fantulin\nHead of R&D @\nAisot Technologies AG\nPrivatdozent @\nETH Zürich\nWorking at the interface of\nFinancial Machine Learning & Complexity Science\n2016 - 2017\nSenior Copywriter\nFabozzi Studio:\nThis is your CV description. Concisely describe your specific role, degree, certification or personal experience.\nMake sure to include relevant skills, accomplishments & milestones gained.\nComplexity Science\nselected\nresearch\n\"Generalization of neural network models for complex network dynamics\"\nCommunication Physics 2024\nFinancial Machine Learning\nselected\nresearch\n\"A Dynamic Regime-Switching Model Using Gated Recurrent Straight-Through Units\",\nJournal of Financial Data Science (2024)\nTeaching\nMachine Learning for Finance and Complex Systems\nLecturer, Spring 2025\nEU projects participation\nSoBigData++ project\nSoBigData\nproject: the European Research Infrastructure for Big Data and Social Mining.\nMultiplex\nproject: Foundational Research on MULTI-level comPLEX networks and systems\nFOC project\n: Forecasting Financial Crisis\ne-LICO project:\nAn e-Laboratory for Interdisciplinary Collaborative Research in Data Mining and Data-Intensive Science\nEducation & positions\nBachelor of Science in Computing\nFaculty of Electrical Engineering and Computing\n, Croatia, Zagreb\nDate 2005-2008\nMaster of Science in Computing\nFaculty of Electrical Engineering and Computing, Croatia, Zagreb,\nLBCB\ngroup\nDate 2008-2010\nPhD in Computer Science (Complex Systems)\nInstitute Rudjer Boskovic\n, Croatia, Zagreb,\nLaboratory for Machine Learning and Knowledge Representations\nDate 2010-2016\nRobert Koch Institute, Berlin, Oct-Nov 2013​​\nPostdoctoral researcher in Complexity & Data Science\nSwiss Federal Institute of Technology (\nETH\n), Zurich,\nCOSS\ngroup, Switzerland, f\nrom 2016-2019\nSenior Scientist\nSwiss Federal Institute of Technology (\nETH\n), Zurich,\nCOSS\ngroup, Switzerland, f\nrom 2019\n​\nVisiting research associate\nCourant Institute of Mathematical Sciences,\nNew York, 2019 - 2023\n​\nSupervisor & Panel member\nPhD Program in Data Science, Scuola Normale Superiore, Pisa\n2019-2023\n​\nPrivatdozent\nETH Zurich, from 2024\nML for Finance & Complex Systems\n​\nHead of Research\nAisot Technologies AG\n​\nComplex systems\nFinancial Machine Learning\nComplexity science (\ncomplex systems\n) studies systems and problems that are composed of many components which may interact with each other in a dynamic and non-linear way, and give rise to its collective behavior.\nFinancial Machine Learning uses methods & algorithms from statistics, machine learning and other disciplines (economics, physics, mathematics) to extract insights from financial data. In particular, I am interested in high frequency finance, volatility, portfolio optimization & large language modelling for finance.\nLinkedin\nbottom of page"
  },
  "serper::Christos-Alexandros Psomas": {
    "homepage": "https://www.alexpsomas.com/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": null,
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "Alex Psomas\nSearch this site\nEmbedded Files\nSkip to main content\nSkip to navigation\nAlex Psomas\nAlex Psomas\nAssistant Professor\nDepartment of Computer Science\nPurdue University\nHall of Data Science and AI (DSAI) 2144\nemail: ''apsomas'' at ''purdue'' dot ''edu''\nDBLP\n|\nGoogle Scholar\nAbout\nMy name is Alexandros Psomas (I mostly go by Alex). I am an Assistant Professor in the Department of Computer Science at\nPurdue University\n. I am also affiliated with the Center for Education and Research in Information Assurance and Security (\nCERIAS\n).\nMy research focuses on the intersection of Computer Science and Economics. I apply tools and insights from computer science to study problems in a variety of economic environments. I am broadly interested in algorithmic economics, artificial intelligence, machine learning, computational social choice and mechanism design, as well as algorithmic game theory and theoretical computer science in general. I am very interested in projects that involve both theory and real-world applications. Examples include collaborations with non-profit organizations such as\n412 Food Rescue\n,\nthe\nIndy Hunger Network\n, and the\nFood Finders Food Bank\n.\nI completed my PhD in May 2017 in the\nTheory of Computation\ngroup at\nUC Berkeley\n, where I was extremely fortunate to be advised by\nChristos Papadimitriou\n. I have worked as a Visiting Researcher in the market algorithms group in Google Research (Mountain View) during the Spring and Summer of 2020. I spent Fall 2019 as a Research Fellow at the Simons Institute for the Theory of Computing. Prior to that I spent two wonderful years (2017-2019) as a postdoctoral researcher in the\nComputer Science Department\nat\nCarnegie Mellon University\n, hosted by\nAriel Procaccia\n.\nMy research is funded by an award from the\nHerbert Simon Family Foundation\n,\na\nGoogle Research Scholar Award\n, a Google AI for Social Good award, a\nMEGA-ACE grant\nfrom the\nAlgorand Foundation\n, and the\nNSF CAREER Award\n.\nNEWS\nJune 2025:\nYair\nand I are organizing a\ntutorial\non applied fair division in EC 2025!\nApril 2025: I was a guest on an episode of\n``This is Purdue,''\nPurdue's official podcast. Many thanks to Kate Young for the invitation!\nNov 2024: Our work with IHN was featured in\nPurdue University's News\n!\nOct 2024: Our paper, \"Automating Food Drop: The Power of Two Choices for Dynamic and Fair Food Allocation,\" with\nMarios\nand\nParitosh\nwon the\nbest student paper award\nin\nEAAMO 2024\n!\nApril 2024: Talking about\ndynamic fair division and our work with the\nIndy Hunger Network\nat\nBrown's CS Theory Seminar\n(\nvideo\n) and\nMIT's Algorithms & Complexity Seminar\n.\nApril 2024: We have launched our automated version of\nFoodDrop\n!\nMarch 2024: Talking about dynamic fair division and our work with the\nIndy Hunger Network\nin the\nMathematics for Humanity Learning Seminar\n.\nSept 2023: Our work on combating food insecurity using artificial intelligence was\ncovered\nby\nIOWA PBS\nand\nPurdue University News\n!\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse\nPage details\nPage updated\nGoogle Sites\nReport abuse"
  },
