  "serper::Yu-Neng Chuang": {
    "homepage": "https://ynchuang.github.io/",
    "emails": [
      "ynchuang@rice.edu"
    ],
    "github": "https://github.com/ynchuang",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/n08041134",
    "homepage_text": "Yu-Neng's Homepage\nYu-Neng's Homepage\nDuncan Hall, 3014\nRice University\nynchuang [at] rice [dot] edu\nAbout Me\nHowdy! I’m Yu-Neng Chuang, a final-year Ph.D. candidate from the Department of\nComputer Science at Rice University\n, co-advised by Dr.\nXia “Ben” Hu\nand Dr.\nVladimir Braverman\n. Before that, I received my bachelor’s degree in Mathematics and my master’s degree in Computer Science, both from National ChengChi University.\nI aim to build reliable and efficient LLM systems through LLM post-training techniques such as efficient long-context reasoning, uncertainty-guided routing, and multimodal agent systems. Currently, I am working on post-training and reinforcement learning for LLM reasoning and multimodal LLM agents.\nI was a research intern at Google Deepmind\n, was a research intern at Apple\n, and was a research intern at Samsung Reserach America\n. I am currently working on industrial collaboration project with Meta AI\n.\n🔥 I am actively seeking full-time Research Scientist/Engineer opportunities starting in May 2026!\nNews\nJan. 2026: One papers have accepted by EACL 2025!\nNov. 2025: Three papers have accepted by NeurIPS 2025!\nAug. 2025: Three papers have accepted by EMNLP 2025!\nMay 2025: I started my internship as a research intern at Google DeepMind!\nMay 2025: Checkout our latest survey on\nEfficient LLM reasoning\n! Feel free to\nUPVOTE\nMay 2025: One paper has accepted by KDD 2025. See you in Toronto!\nMay 2025: Two papers have accepted by ACL 2025. See you in Vienna!\nMay 2025: One paper has accepted by ICML 2025.\nJan. 2025: One paper has accepted by NAACL 2025. See you in Albuquerque!\nNov. 2024: I am honor to received the Ken Kennedy Institute 2024/25 Andrew Ladd Memorial Fellowship!\nSep. 2024: Two papers have been accepted by EMNLP 2024\nSep. 2024: One paper has been selected as the oral paper (10% of oral accept rate) at Baylearn Machine Learning Symposium 2024, held by Apple, Google, UC Berkeley, etc.\nAug. 2024: Checkout our recent benchmarking works on\nTime series foundation models\n,\nKV Cache compression\n, and\nLLM evaluation\nJul. 2024: One paper accepted by TKDD 2024\nMay 2024: I started my internship at Apple!\nMay 2024: One paper has been accepted by ICML 2024\nMar. 2024: Our paper,\nThe Science of Detecting LLM-Generated Text\n, has been accepted for a cover publication in the April 2024 issue of Communications of the ACM (CACM)!\nMar. 2024: Two papers have been accepted by NAACL 2024\nJan. 2024: One paper has been accepted by the Journal of Biomedical Informatics\nOct. 2023: Our paper has been selected as CIKM 2023 Best Demo Paper Honorable Mention!\nMay 2023: I started my internship at Samsung Research America!\nJan. 2023: One paper has been accepted by ICLR 2023\nJul. 2022: One paper has been accepted by ICML 2022\nFeel free to contact me by any means."
  },
  "serper::Raphael J. L. Townshend": {
    "homepage": "https://raphael.tc.com/",
    "emails": [],
    "github": "https://github.com/raphtown",
    "linkedin": "https://www.linkedin.com/in/raphael-townshend-9154962a/",
    "homepage_text": "Search\nRaphaël John Lamarre Townshend\nFounder & CEO\nAtomic AI\nBiography\nRaphaël is the founder and CEO of Atomic AI (we are hiring!), an artificial intelligence-driven startup developing the cutting-edge fusion of machine learning and structural biology to unlock RNA drug discovery.  He studied his PhD in the Stanford AI Lab, and his areas of focus include machine learning, RNA and structural biology, high performance computing, and computer vision.  More broadly, he is interested in connecting the worlds of biology and computation in novel ways.\nInterests\nMachine Learning\nStructural Biology\nRNA Biology\nHigh Performance Computing\nComputer Vision\nEducation\nPhD in Computer Science, 2014-2020\nStanford University\nBS in Electrical Engineering and Computer Science, 2010-2014\nUniversity of California, Berkeley\nFeatured\nRaphaël John Lamarre Townshend\n,\nStephan Eismann\n,\nAndrew M. Watkins\n,\nRamya Rangan\n,\nMaria Karelina\n,\nRhiju Das\n,\nRon O. Dror\nAugust 2021\nScience\nGeometric Deep Learning of RNA Structure\nRNA molecules fold into complex three-dimensional shapes that are difficult to determine experimentally or predict computationally. We introduce a deep-learning method that significantly improves prediction of 3D RNA structures. Understanding these structures may aid in the discovery of drugs for currently untreatable diseases.\nPDF\nCite\nScience Perspective\nStanford News\nChemical & Engineering News\nGenetic Engineering & Biotechnology News\nExperience\nFounder & CEO\nAtomic AI\nMay 2021 –\n            \n              Present\nCalifornia\nUsing AI to enable the rational design of new molecules and medicines.  We are hiring!\nResearch Scientist Intern\nDeepMind\nJun 2019 –\n            \n              Nov 2019\nLondon\nPursued new research in artificial intelligence and\nstructural biology on the Science: AlphaFold team.\nHead TA - CS229\nStanford University\nSep 2018 –\n            \n              Dec 2018\nCalifornia\nLed teaching staff of 30 in instructing over 750 students in\nStanford’s machine learning course.  Designed and lectured new course\nmaterial.\nPhD Fellow\nStanford University\nSep 2014 –\n            \n              Oct 2020\nCalifornia\nThesis: Geometric Learning of Biomolecular Structure. Lead research group at the\nintersection of machine learning and structural biology.\nSoftware Engineering Intern\nGoogle\nJun 2014 –\n            \n              Sep 2014\nCalifornia\nKnowledge: Search team.\nUndergraduate Student Instructor\nUniversity of California, Berkeley\nJun 2012 –\n            \n              Aug 2012\nCalifornia\nTaught Machine Structures course to section and lab of 30\nundergraduates.  Guest lectured on MapReduce to class of 100.\nUndergradute Research Assistant\nUniversity of California, Berkeley\nMay 2012 –\n            \n              Jun 2014\nCalifornia\nComputer vision for large-scale geolocation.\nRoot Staff Member and Director\nUniversity of California, Berkeley\nAug 2011 –\n            \n              Jun 2014\nCalifornia\nVolunteer high-level administrator for computing facility\nwith over 7000 active student users.\nPublications\nRaphaël John Lamarre Townshend\n,\nMartin Vogele\n,\nPatricia A. Suriana\n,\nAlexander Derry\n,\nAlexander Powers\n,\nYianni Laloudakis\n,\nSidhika Balachandar\n,\nBrandon Anderson\n,\nStephan Eismann\n,\nRisi Kondor\n,\nRuss B. Altman\n,\nRon O. Dror\nDecember 2021\nIn NeurIPS 2021 Datasets and Benchmarks (Best Paper)\nATOM3D: Tasks On Molecules in Three Dimensions\nWe build a “dataset of datasets” to help explore the possibilities of three-dimensional molecular learning.\nPDF\nCite\nCode\nPoster\nVideo\nBest Paper Award\nRaphaël John Lamarre Townshend\n,\nStephan Eismann\n,\nAndrew M. Watkins\n,\nRamya Rangan\n,\nMaria Karelina\n,\nRhiju Das\n,\nRon O. Dror\nAugust 2021\nScience\nGeometric Deep Learning of RNA Structure\nRNA molecules fold into complex three-dimensional shapes that are difficult to determine experimentally or predict computationally. We introduce a deep-learning method that significantly improves prediction of 3D RNA structures. Understanding these structures may aid in the discovery of drugs for currently untreatable diseases.\nPDF\nCite\nScience Perspective\nStanford News\nChemical & Engineering News\nGenetic Engineering & Biotechnology News\nBowen Jing\n,\nStephan Eismann\n,\nPatricia A. Suriana\n,\nRaphaël John Lamarre Townshend\n,\nRon O. Dror\nMay 2021\nIn ICLR 2021 (Oral)\nLearning from Protein Structure with Geometric Vector Perceptrons\nWe combine graph and geometric aspects of biomolecular structure within a single unified framework.\nPDF\nCite\nNaomi R. Latorraca\n,\nMatthieu Masureel\n,\nScott A. Hollingsworth\n,\nFranziska M. Heydenreich\n,\nCarl-Mikael Suomivuori\n,\nConnor Brinton\n,\nRaphaël John Lamarre Townshend\n,\nMichel Bouvier\n,\nBrian K. Kobilka\n,\nRon O. Dror\nDecember 2020\nCell\nHow GPCR Phosphorylation Patterns Orchestrate Arrestin-Mediated Signaling\nWe investigate the role of GPCR phosphorylation in modulating arrestin binding and conformation, revealing the structural basis for the long-standing “barcode” hypothesis.\nPDF\nCite\nStephan Eismann\n,\nRaphaël John Lamarre Townshend\n,\nNathaniel Thomas\n,\nMilind Jagota\n,\nBowen Jing\n,\nRon O. Dror\nDecember 2020\nProteins\nHierarchical, rotation‐equivariant neural networks to select structural models of protein complexes\nWe use a novel class of neural network architectures to accurately predict the structures of 3D protein complexes.\nPDF\nCite\nSee all publications\nAccomplish­ments\nForbes 30 under 30\nForbes\nDec 2022\nListed on Forbes 30 under 30.\nGeneral Chair of Machine Learning for Structural Biology\nNeural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)\nDec 2020\nProposed and led inaugural workshop at premiere machine learning conference.\nScience Graduate Student Research Fellowship\nDepartment of Energy\nNov 2019\n          \n          – Nov 2020\nTo pursue large-scale machine learning on biomolecules.\nGraduate Research Fellowship\nNational Science Foundation\nSep 2015\n          \n          – Sep 2018\nSupports outstanding graduate students in science.\nSchool of Engineering Fellowship\nStanford University\nSep 2014\n          \n          – Sep 2015\nOffered to top entering PhD students.\nACM international Collegiate Programming Contest\nUniversity of California, Berkeley\nOct 2013\nPlaced on ACM ICPC team.\nHonors Degree Program\nUniversity of California, Berkeley\nMar 2013\n          \n          – Jun 2014\nConcentration in Statistical Learning.\nEta Kappa Nu\nUniversity of California, Berkeley\nDec 2011\nElected to Undegraduate Honors Society.\nProjects\n*\nAll\nGeometry\nComputer Vision\nMisc\nA Review of Science\nAscertaining whether we are done with Science\nForward\nPort forwarding on compute clusters.  Useful for the likes of Jupyter Notebooks, Tensorboard…\nPool++\nPool augmented with computer vision, LIDAR, and lasers.\nSlides\nBuilding Detection\nFinding buildings in satellite imagery.\nPDF\nExpression Recognition\nClassification of facial expressions.\nPDF\nFutureHand\nHomebrew IMU USB device for 6-DOF control.\nPDF\nMoonWeasel\n3D multiplayer space flight simulator.\nCode\nContact\nMessage\nConnect\nCite\n×\nCopy\nDownload"
  },
