  "serper::Zhenhui": {
    "homepage": "http://www.zhenhuiyuan.net/",
    "emails": [
      "zhenhui.yuan@warwick.ac.uk"
    ],
    "github": "https://github.com/ConnectedRoboticsLab",
    "linkedin": null,
    "homepage_text": "﻿\nZhenhui Yuan\nZhenhui Yuan (袁振珲), PhD\nAssistant Professor, University of Warwick, UK\nConsultant Member, Institute for Applied & Translational Technologies in Surgery, University Hospitals Coventry & Warwickshire/NHS Trust, UK\nIEEE Senior Member\nzhenhui.yuan@warwick.ac.uk\n#Connected and Collaborative Robotics Lab (CCR Lab)\nAbout Me\nPhD Scholarship\nOpen Position: unavailable now.\nOther scholarships:\nChina Scholarship Council - University of Warwick Scholarship\n(deadline: December 2025)\nSchool of Engineering MPhil/PhD Scholarship Scheme\n(deadline: December 2025)\nChancellors' International Scholarships\n(deadline: December 2025)\nWarwick-Shanghai Jiaotong University Joint PhD Programme (4 years)\n(deadline: Dec 2025)\nI am very happy to host joint-PhD/Postdoc/Visiting Researchers. Please feel free to reach out!\nNEWS!\nco-Chair, 4th workshop on \"6G-empowered Robotic Vehicles for Sustainable Development\" in\nIEEE Vehicular Technology Conference (VTC),\n9–12 June 2026, Nice, France.\nVeSUS website\nLead Guest Editor,\nIEEE Internet of Things Magazine\nSpecial Issue on Edge AI for Internet of Robotic Things, deadline: May 15th, 2025.\nCall-for-Papers\nLead Chair, 3rd workshop on \"6G-empowered Robotic Vehicles for Sustainable Development\" in\nIEEE International Conference on Communications (ICC),\n8–12 June 2025, Montreal, Canada.\nVeSUS website\nLead Chair, 2nd workshop on \"6G-empowered Robotic Vehicles for Sustainable Development\" in\nIEEE Vehicular Technology Conference (VTC),\nSingapore, 2024.\nVeSUS website\nLead Guest Editor,\nIEEE Internet of Things Journal\n(IF:10.238, Q1) Special Issue on Integrated Sensing, Computing and Communication for Internet of Robotic Things, deadline: Apr 15th, 2024.\nCall-for-Papers\nLead Guest Editor,\nIEEE Network\n(IF:9.3, Q1) Special Issue on Internet of Robots, deadline: Oct 15th, 2023.\nCall-for-Papers\nLead Chair, 1st workshop on \"6G-empowered Robotic Vehicles for Sustainable Development\" in\nIEEE Vehicular Technology Conference (VTC),\nItaly, 2023.\nVeSUS website\nTeaching\nES96T “Advanced Wireless Systems and Networks”, University of Warwick\nES9ZC “Communication and Information Engineering Design Group Project”, University of Warwick\nBIO\nDr. Zhenhui Yuan leads the Connected and Collaborated Robotics Lab (CCR Lab) where he focuses on applying AI and advanced networking technologies to enable seamless robot-to-robot and robot-to-human collaboration. He received a BEng in Software Engineering from Wuhan University, China, in 2008, and a PhD in Electronic Engineering from Dublin City University, Ireland, in 2012. He was a postdoctoral researcher (2012-2014) funded by Ericsson and Enterprise Ireland, and a visiting scientist at the Network Research Lab at UCLA (2014). Between 2014 and 2015, he was the 3GPP Delegate and Senior Research Engineer on 5G at Huawei Technologies (Shanghai). From 2015 to 2019, he was the co-Founder and CTO of RobSense Technology (Hangzhou, China), a startup producing mobile robots for industry. RobSense has secured venture investment exceeding 15 million RMB (~£1.5m). He was an adjunct associate professor at the Ministry of Education Key Lab of RF Circuits and Systems, Hangzhou Dianzi University, China during 2015-2019. He was a Senior Lecturer at Northumbria University (UK) from 2020 to 2023 and is currently an Assistant Professor at the University of Warwick.\r\n\r\nHe currently serves as the Consulting Member (on Telerobotics in Healthcare) at the Institute for Applied & Translational Technologies in Surgery, University Hospitals Coventry and Warwickshire/NHS Trust. He is a Voting Member of the IEEE P1954 Working Group on UAV communications. He has led and participated research projects funded by British Academy, Horizon EU and NSFC China. He was the Leading Guest Editor for special issues at IEEE Network, IEEE IoT-Magazine, and IEEE IoT-Journal, and an Associate Editor for IEEE ACCESS, IEEE Transactions on Consumer Electronics, and the Journal of Pervasive Computing & Communications. He is the founding Chair of the VeSUS workshop (\"6G-empowered Robotic Vehicles for Sustainable Development\") which has been successfully held in IEEE VTC 2023/2024 and IEEE ICC 2025.\nProjects\nco-I\n\"MUXNET:Multisensory XR-based Telesurgery\", The Academy of Medical Sciences, UK, £21,030, 2025-2026.\nPI\n, \"A Live Holographic Communication System for Telesurgery\", Research Development Awards, University of Warwick, 2024-2025 (£9,000).\nPI\n, \"Multimodal Simulation and Decision-making for Future Transport Network\", British Academy Pump Priming Award, 2024 (£9,000).\nCo-I\n, \"Electric Vehicles pOint Location optimization via VEhicular communications (EVOLVE)\", Horizon Europe MSCA Staff Exchange, UK, 2022-2026 (£92K).\nPI\n, \"Communications Subsystems Design for Drone Swarm\", Qianjiang Talented Programme (#QJD1602018), P.R.China, Jan 2017-Dec 2019 (50,000 RMB).\nPI\n, \"Software Defined Routing for Unmanned Aerial Vehicles Swarm\", NSFC (#61601159), P.R.China, Jan 2017-Dec 2019 (260,000 RMB).\nPI\n, \"Drone, Car, Cabinet: Smart 'Last-mile' Delivery\", Zhejiang Open Foundation of the Most Important Subjects, Oct 2015 - Oct 2016 (50,000 RMB).\nPI\n, \"Augmented Reality Video Streaming Service: Protocol and Prototype\", Dublin City University Performance Enhancing Initiatives, Nov. 2013-Oct. 2014 (2,500 euro).\nPackage Leader\n, \"E-Stream: Innovative Performance Management System for HetNet Environments\", Ericsson/Enterprise Ireland, 2012-2014, (400k Euro).\nConnected and Collaborative Robotics Lab (CCR Lab)\nSwarm Robotics (#Robot-to-Robot)\n[Figure: Our Drone Swarm Show at Westlake, Hangzhou]\nWe focus on advancing swarm robotics by exploring the fundamental scaling laws that govern collective intelligence. The core objective is to\r\n                        understand how system-level performance—such as the accuracy, robustness, and timeliness of collective model inference—scales with key\r\n                        inputs including communication resource, onboard computing capacity, swarm size, data heterogeneity, and energy constraints.\r\n                        Inspired by well-known theories such as ant colony behavior, multi-agent reinforcement learning, and federated learning, we study how robots can learn to coordinate,\r\n                        share information, and adapt their actions as environments and network conditions change. Ultimately, our research aim to provide principled design\r\n                        guidelines for scalable, resilient, and energy-efficient collective intelligence in real-world swarm robotic systems.\nTelerobotics (#Human-to-Robot)\n[Figure: Teleoperation at CCR Lab, Warwick. Acknowledgement: Zexin Deng]\nTelerobotics offers significant potential for performing complex tasks in hazardous and extreme environments from a distance.\r\n                When augmented with mixed reality interfaces, telerobotic systems can create a strong sense of physical presence, enabling users to interact with\r\n                remote environments more intuitively. Despite this promise, many challenges remain unresolved. The effectiveness of telerobotic systems depends\r\n                critically on low-latency communication across diverse scenarios (e.g. <100 ms for remote surgery). Moreover, the combined effects of network delay, task complexity, shared control strategies, and human operator acceptance and experience\r\n                are not yet fully understood. Addressing these challenges calls for the development of unified human–robot interaction models, robust communication\r\n                frameworks, and standardised evaluation benchmarks.\nToolkits\nTeleSim v1.0\n[\nGithub\n]\r\n          [\nPaper\n]\nTeleSim is a hardware-in-the-loop testbed that evaluates the performance of\r\n        telerobotic systems under varying network conditions.\nOperating System: Ubuntu 20.04\nOMNeT++ Version: OMNeT++ 6.0\nRobot Arm: Ufactory Lite 6\nVideo Streaming Emulator\n[\nGithub\n]\nReal video sequences are transmitted through a virtual network simulated in OMNeT++, where network impairments are introduced.\nOperating System: Ubuntu 20.04\nOMNeT++ Version: OMNeT++ 6.0\nINET Version: INET 4.4\nHardware-in-the-Loop 5G Emulator\n[\nGithub\n]\nA hardware-in-the-loop network emulator allowing programmable control of\r\n        network conditions and topology. The impact of variable 5G conditions on\r\n        vehicular services such as video streaming can be evaluated realistically.\n5G base station: Simu5G (open-source)\nOperating System: Ubuntu 20.04\nOMNeT++ Version: OMNeT++ 6.0.1\nINET Version: INET 4.3.9\nEasySwarm: Ground Control Station for Drone Swarm\n[\nGithub\n]\nA ground control software designed to manage and coordinate a swarm of robots,\r\n        supporting both drones and rovers. The system provides\r\n        centralized monitoring and commanding, enabling operators to supervise multi-robot missions,\nOperating System: Windows 10\nRobSense Drone R450\nRobSense SwarmLink\nDrone platform\nProducts: RobSense Technologies (Hangzhou)\nI am the inventor and product manager of SwarmLink Radio, which is included by\nArdupilot.org\nSwarmLink radio (LoRa)\nFlight controller (FPGA+CPU)\n5G drone platform\nFlight Controller\nServices\nMember, Institute for Applied & Translational Technologies in Surgery, University Hospitals Coventry & Warwickshire/NHS Trust, UK, 2025-\nLead Guest Editor,\nIEEE Internet of Things Journal\n(IF:10.238, Q1) Special Issue on Integrated Sensing, Computing and Communication for Internet of Robotic Things, 2024.\nGeneral Chair, IEEE VTC-Spring workshop (\nVeSUS\n), 2024\nLead Guest Editor,\nIEEE Network\n(IF:9.3, Q1) Special Issue on Internet of Robots, 2023.\nGeneral Chair, IEEE VTC-Spring workshop (\nVeSUS\n), 2023\nVoting member, IEEE P.1954 (networking of UAVs), 2022-\nGuest Lecturer, “How is IoT changing our society”, IKADO, Indonesia, 2022\nBest Supervisor, 1st prize, National Postgraduate Electronics Design Contest: cloud-based 3D printing, 2018\nBest Supervisor, 2nd prize, National Postgraduate Electronics Design Contest: Firefighting with UAVs, 2018\nWorkshop Chair, IEEE 5G...[truncated]"
  },
